00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:52.500
So, today I am drawing on my research

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:54.530
on the Euro-African border in Morocco

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:58.000
to make the case that race and racism

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:00.750
and especially in the
context that I investigate,

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:05.080
Blackness and anti-Blackness
are central to determinations

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:07.960
about whose mobility is subject to control

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:09.950
and how that works.

00:04:09.950 --> 00:04:11.260
So, before I go any further though,

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:13.370
I wanna unpack a few terms.

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:15.990
So, when I talk about
the Euro-African border,

00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:18.640
I'm speaking of the
network of governments,

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:22.620
law enforcement agencies,
humanitarian organizations,

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:26.300
defense contractors, smuggling
rings and other entities

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:28.550
that are implicated in the control

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:32.850
of people's migratory movements
between Africa and Europe.

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:35.750
And this network has grown out of the EU

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:40.520
and individual member states'
shift in the mid to late 1990s

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:43.560
toward outsourcing immigration control

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:47.400
to neighboring countries whose
participation is enlisted

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:50.853
through various diplomatic
and financial incentives.

00:04:52.175 --> 00:04:53.560
And just a little side note,

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:56.670
Nora El Qadim is a
person who problematizes

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:59.640
maybe the incentivization
of the externalized borders,

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:01.023
so, if you wanna know more about that

00:05:01.023 --> 00:05:02.823
I encourage you to look up her work.

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:06.790
Most scholars call this
sort of outsourced border,

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:09.900
the externalized border,
or border externalization

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:12.713
and you'll hear me refer to
it in this talk in that way.

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:15.490
So, in the externalized border,

00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:17.740
walls and fences certainly play a role

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:21.000
and definitely draw much
of the media attention,

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.820
but the border signifies
something much more expansive.

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:27.310
It's a political and economic complex,

00:05:27.310 --> 00:05:31.280
whose reason for being depends
upon the criminalization

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:35.130
of certain groups of people's
cross-border movements.

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:37.560
Further, borders aren't
just inner barriers,

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:40.190
be they physical,
institutional, algorithmic,

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:43.320
legal or social, but active processes

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:46.290
of inscribing, inclusions and exclusions

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:48.230
throughout social space.

00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:50.640
So, in this way, borders are productive.

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:53.120
They aren't just about
restricting, or inhibiting,

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:56.663
but they're generating
relations, subjects and spaces.

00:05:58.118 --> 00:06:00.260
So, when I talk about
race, I'm not referring

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:02.510
to unchanging identities, or categories

00:06:02.510 --> 00:06:05.680
whose meaning is fixed
across time and space,

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:08.500
but instead I understand race
as a category of difference

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:12.260
produced through ongoing
processes of racialization

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:14.770
with local and transregional,

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:17.860
as well as historical
and contemporary valence.

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:20.500
So, what this means is it
being Black in Morocco,

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:24.220
for example, is not the same,
even though it resonates with

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:26.060
being Black in the United States.

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:27.750
And as we'll see in a bit,

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:29.660
neither is being white in Morocco

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:33.030
the same as being white in
the U.S. or in Mauritania,

00:06:33.030 --> 00:06:35.983
or in South Africa, or
in countries in Europe.

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:39.120
When I argue that race
and especially Blackness

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:40.890
is central to mobility control

00:06:40.890 --> 00:06:42.490
in the Euro-African borderlands,

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:45.487
it means it isn't just that the
border targets Black people,

00:06:45.487 --> 00:06:48.690
but that it produces people as Black,

00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:52.530
collapsing their multiple
identities into a single figure

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:55.160
of the Black, or African migrant.

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:57.530
And further, it assigns
meanings about Blackness

00:06:57.530 --> 00:07:00.050
that legitimize the
violence against people

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:03.090
conceived as fundamentally out of place,

00:07:03.090 --> 00:07:04.830
not just in Morocco or Europe,

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:06.533
but within the realm of the human.

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:10.580
And by the way, slides and pictures,

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:12.500
so you don't have to look at
me the whole time, are coming,

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:15.710
but I just wanna get
this introduction first.

00:07:15.710 --> 00:07:18.490
So, to unpack this, I'm
gonna tell you a story

00:07:18.490 --> 00:07:21.280
about a conversation I had
in the midst of field work

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:25.270
in 2017 and 2018 among West
and Central African migrants

00:07:25.270 --> 00:07:28.200
who'd found themselves
stranded in Morocco.

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:30.650
The story points to the ways
that the Euoro-African border

00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:32.120
is not just reorganizing

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:34.750
transnational economic relationships,

00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:37.630
or challenging notions of
sovereignty and territory,

00:07:37.630 --> 00:07:41.170
which are concerns that
border scholars have taken up,

00:07:41.170 --> 00:07:44.320
but is reworking the
social and significant ways

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:47.650
across Morocco and, I
would argue by extension,

00:07:47.650 --> 00:07:50.250
throughout the Euro-African borderlands.

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:52.870
My approach builds on
decades of feminist critique

00:07:52.870 --> 00:07:55.100
of scale and ethnographic research

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:58.560
in order to understand how
the bodily and the global

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:01.750
mutually constitute places and people.

00:08:01.750 --> 00:08:04.240
Thus thinking about the
border ethnic graphically,

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:08.100
requires attention to the
daily embodied experiences

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:11.910
of people who encounter,
reproduce and transgress it.

00:08:11.910 --> 00:08:14.510
And in my work, I argue
that migrants are implicated

00:08:14.510 --> 00:08:16.440
in all of those actions.

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:19.370
So, through a relational
lens, I trace connections

00:08:19.370 --> 00:08:20.830
rather than distinctions

00:08:20.830 --> 00:08:23.220
that characterize the border's operations

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:27.230
and migrants' experiences
in specific places

00:08:27.230 --> 00:08:29.060
within, what Ruben Andersson calls,

00:08:29.060 --> 00:08:32.930
the extended field sites,
the Euro-African border.

00:08:32.930 --> 00:08:36.573
All right, so I'm gonna
share my screen with you now.

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:41.933
All right.

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:46.240
So, I conducted most of my
field work in Fes and Meknes

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:49.790
which are two Central Moroccan
cities that have been host

00:08:49.790 --> 00:08:53.360
to growing numbers of West and
Central African populations.

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:55.820
This is in part due to
the Moroccan state shift

00:08:55.820 --> 00:08:58.440
a few years previous to this research,

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:00.760
from a practice of apprehending migrants

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:03.390
in the Northern part of
Morocco, which I refer to

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:06.000
as the frontier region,
or the frontier zone,

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:09.570
near fences that separate Morocco
from two Spanish enclaves,

00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:13.590
Melilla and Ceuta, Ceuta
that you can see on the map.

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:15.560
Formerly, the state had rounded up folks

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:17.150
attempting to breach the fences,

00:09:17.150 --> 00:09:19.200
or cross the Mediterranean on boats,

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:21.040
or sometimes just rounded people up

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:24.250
who were phenotypically
Black in the Northern cities

00:09:24.250 --> 00:09:27.980
and dropped them off in the
desert at the Algerian border.

00:09:27.980 --> 00:09:30.170
This practice has been widely denounced

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:33.340
by human rights associations
in Morocco and in Europe,

00:09:33.340 --> 00:09:36.810
and was replaced as part
of a humanitarian reform

00:09:36.810 --> 00:09:38.663
of Morocco's border policy.

00:09:39.530 --> 00:09:41.010
So, what replaced it instead

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:43.200
were these internal deportations

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:47.960
in which any Black non-citizen,
in the words of one report,

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:50.280
in the frontier zone
were still rounded up,

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:52.110
but rather than dropping
them in the desert,

00:09:52.110 --> 00:09:53.760
they were (indiscernible)

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:56.450
to cities in the country's interior.

00:09:56.450 --> 00:09:58.460
This practice of pushing people back

00:09:58.460 --> 00:10:00.400
from the Northern border zone

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:02.470
to more remote Southern locations,

00:10:02.470 --> 00:10:05.520
mirrors the EU's region-wide strategy

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:08.310
of pushing migrants from its
Southern and Eastern borders

00:10:08.310 --> 00:10:11.750
into countries like
Morocco, Libya and Turkey.

00:10:11.750 --> 00:10:13.910
These are countries that
have fewer resources

00:10:13.910 --> 00:10:16.043
and infrastructures to accommodate them.

00:10:19.530 --> 00:10:21.980
I think part of this
border externalization

00:10:21.980 --> 00:10:23.830
and the pushing back that is happening,

00:10:23.830 --> 00:10:25.090
or creating a buffer zone,

00:10:25.090 --> 00:10:27.370
is something that just
keeps getting replicated,

00:10:27.370 --> 00:10:29.863
replicated on a smaller and smaller scale.

00:10:31.830 --> 00:10:36.020
So, once in Fes, or Meknes,
migrants were often dropped off

00:10:36.020 --> 00:10:38.040
near the train station, or bus station

00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:41.210
and encampments were
established in empty lots

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:42.360
adjacent to them.

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:44.310
And this is true, not
just in Fes or Meknes

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:46.860
but in many cities across the country.

00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:49.080
Many people newly arrived
from the North state

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:51.500
in these encampments, are
moved to overcrowded rooms

00:10:51.500 --> 00:10:53.960
in working class neighborhoods
where they survive

00:10:53.960 --> 00:10:58.200
by begging on the street,
or engaged in informal work.

00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:02.210
One effect of this policy, was
it heightened the visibility

00:11:02.210 --> 00:11:04.160
of West and Central African migrants

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:07.500
on Moroccan city streets,
reinforcing the link

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:12.360
between Blackness and unauthorized,
or undesired migration.

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:15.240
This heightened visibility
and conflation of Blackness

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:18.170
with illegality, impacted
different communities

00:11:18.170 --> 00:11:20.730
within Morocco in several ways.

00:11:20.730 --> 00:11:24.200
First, it conceal the fact
that many of the people

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:27.580
migrating to Europe from
Morocco without papers,

00:11:27.580 --> 00:11:30.150
were in fact Moroccan nationals.

00:11:30.150 --> 00:11:32.390
Second, it reframed how people perceived

00:11:32.390 --> 00:11:36.290
the presence of many Sub-Saharan
students with legal papers.

00:11:36.290 --> 00:11:37.670
So, these Sub-Saharan students

00:11:37.670 --> 00:11:40.320
have long been present in Morocco

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:44.030
and actually are brought to
Morocco through a program

00:11:44.030 --> 00:11:45.990
that the state sponsors.

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:48.700
And finally, it's impacted Black Moroccans

00:11:48.700 --> 00:11:51.320
whose belonging has
always been racialized,

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:54.960
but is now being animated by
anti-Black migration politics.

00:11:54.960 --> 00:11:57.410
And that's kind of what
I'm gonna focus on today.

00:11:59.070 --> 00:12:02.020
So, in Fes, I worked closely
with a research partner,

00:12:02.020 --> 00:12:06.150
Freddy, or as he likes
to say, he had a driver.

00:12:06.150 --> 00:12:08.790
Several times a week I would
meet him at the McDonald's

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:11.440
in downtown Fes, where we
would have a quick breakfast

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:13.700
and then spend the next six or so hours

00:12:13.700 --> 00:12:17.310
visiting migrant communities
in the outdoor encampment

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:19.130
adjacent to the train station,

00:12:19.130 --> 00:12:20.933
or in a working class neighborhood.

00:12:21.770 --> 00:12:23.650
Freddy wears lots of hats.

00:12:23.650 --> 00:12:25.760
Himself a former migrant from Cameroon,

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:27.680
he took the land journey from Morocco

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:29.220
when he was a teenager.

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:32.160
He's now a social worker
with a church-based NGO

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:36.900
and with a Moroccan-based NGO
focused on HIV prevention.

00:12:36.900 --> 00:12:39.670
He's also the leader of
the Cameroonian community

00:12:39.670 --> 00:12:42.020
by virtue of his seniority in the country

00:12:42.020 --> 00:12:44.840
and his important positions with NGOs.

00:12:44.840 --> 00:12:47.840
In his social worker role,
he responds to urgent health

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:50.870
and social services needs
of all Black people,

00:12:50.870 --> 00:12:53.040
as he says, in Fes.

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:57.260
People from Cameroon, Nigeria,
Guinea, Mali, Ivory Coast,

00:12:57.260 --> 00:12:58.560
and so on.

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:00.410
His phone never stops ringing,

00:13:00.410 --> 00:13:02.810
rounding up blankets
for newly-arrived people

00:13:02.810 --> 00:13:05.740
dropped off at the camp by
a Moroccan border police,

00:13:05.740 --> 00:13:09.040
calling supervisors to ask
for the release of funds

00:13:09.040 --> 00:13:11.060
so he can pay for prescriptions

00:13:11.060 --> 00:13:14.030
for migrants who are
experiencing hypertension,

00:13:14.030 --> 00:13:17.173
or to purchase insulin for diabetes.

00:13:19.965 --> 00:13:23.040
He's accompanied pregnant
women to their prenatal visits

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:26.920
to provide translation,
identifies bodies at the morgue

00:13:26.920 --> 00:13:29.640
and arranges for a burial
in a pauper's grave,

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:32.200
helps someone arrange
their self-deportation

00:13:33.060 --> 00:13:34.640
and will file applications

00:13:34.640 --> 00:13:37.120
for one-year regularization program

00:13:38.820 --> 00:13:40.750
for mothers and children.

00:13:40.750 --> 00:13:44.210
And as Cameroonian community
leader, he mediates disputes

00:13:44.210 --> 00:13:47.170
between individuals who
visits people in prison,

00:13:47.170 --> 00:13:49.840
and while I was there,
arranged for a sex worker

00:13:49.840 --> 00:13:51.660
who was not getting along with her madam

00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:54.580
to be transferred to a
madam in another city

00:13:54.580 --> 00:13:56.600
so that she could be safe.

00:13:56.600 --> 00:13:58.470
Often when I dropped him
off in the neighborhood

00:13:58.470 --> 00:14:01.160
in the early evening,
he had hours more calls

00:14:01.160 --> 00:14:02.253
and visits to make.

00:14:05.670 --> 00:14:08.780
So, after six, or seven
months as Freddy's chauffeur,

00:14:08.780 --> 00:14:12.240
I took a short vacation to the Tafilalet,

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:14.340
located in the Southeast of the country

00:14:14.340 --> 00:14:16.910
near Morocco's fabled sand dunes.

00:14:16.910 --> 00:14:19.880
The Tafilalet oasis system
runs along the Ziz River

00:14:19.880 --> 00:14:22.580
between the provincial capital of Rachidia

00:14:22.580 --> 00:14:25.870
to the north in Erfoud,
the gateway to the Sahara

00:14:25.870 --> 00:14:29.150
as tourist operators like
to say, to the south.

00:14:29.150 --> 00:14:32.840
The Tafilalet has fascinated
generations of anthropologists

00:14:32.840 --> 00:14:36.520
also for its diverse,
entangled, cultural, religious,

00:14:36.520 --> 00:14:39.973
racial and ethnic makeup, Arabs, Jews,

00:14:41.169 --> 00:14:44.240
Black or Arab Berbers,
descendants of slaves,

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:47.360
so-called white Berbers,
descendants of the prophet,

00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:51.200
the Shorfa, or of holy people, and so on.

00:14:51.200 --> 00:14:53.130
And we can talk more
about that in the Q&amp;A,

00:14:53.130 --> 00:14:55.300
or I can point you to
some excellent resources

00:14:55.300 --> 00:14:58.590
including Hussein Al Lahyan's work.

00:14:58.590 --> 00:15:00.340
So, though I didn't
travel to the Tafilalet

00:15:00.340 --> 00:15:03.490
to study the border, I
encountered it anyway.

00:15:03.490 --> 00:15:06.000
The internal deportations
that it contributed

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:08.230
to more West and Central African migrants

00:15:08.230 --> 00:15:11.040
living in and stranded in Fes and Meknes,

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:13.330
were now being diverted to more remote

00:15:13.330 --> 00:15:16.300
and economically marginal
towns, further south,

00:15:16.300 --> 00:15:18.300
or further in the Moroccan interior,

00:15:18.300 --> 00:15:20.850
including both Rachidia and Erfoud,

00:15:20.850 --> 00:15:22.050
each a short distance away

00:15:22.050 --> 00:15:23.963
from where this picture was taken.

00:15:25.110 --> 00:15:28.210
Indeed, in Fes I met many
people who'd been pushed back

00:15:28.210 --> 00:15:31.110
from the Northern border to
Rachidia, to Beni Mellal,

00:15:31.110 --> 00:15:33.720
or to Tiznit, before making
their way back to Fes,

00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:35.720
or heading back to the Northern frontier

00:15:35.720 --> 00:15:37.370
to try to cross the border again.

00:15:40.020 --> 00:15:41.450
On my visit to the Tafilalet,

00:15:41.450 --> 00:15:43.250
I was chatting with Fatima Zahra,

00:15:43.250 --> 00:15:45.840
an Amazigh or Berber activist,

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:47.440
about my research among migrants

00:15:47.440 --> 00:15:50.210
experiencing these internal deportations

00:15:50.210 --> 00:15:53.330
and how I thought the practice
seemed to be reinforcing,

00:15:53.330 --> 00:15:56.220
or intensifying anti-Black discrimination

00:15:56.220 --> 00:15:59.290
against both migrants and Black Moroccans.

00:15:59.290 --> 00:16:01.810
She responded with the local
version of the argument

00:16:01.810 --> 00:16:05.420
I've heard many times since
moving to Morocco in 2001.

00:16:05.420 --> 00:16:07.720
In the Tafilalet there have
been African immigrants

00:16:07.720 --> 00:16:11.600
for centuries as a result of
the Trans-Saharan slave trade,

00:16:11.600 --> 00:16:13.670
but despite their unfree status,

00:16:13.670 --> 00:16:17.610
they suffered no discrimination
in Moroccan society.

00:16:17.610 --> 00:16:19.780
Pointing to a sepia-toned picture

00:16:19.780 --> 00:16:22.907
of a traditionally garbed
man on the wall, she said,

00:16:22.907 --> 00:16:25.017
"My grandfather owned two slaves

00:16:25.017 --> 00:16:27.490
"and he treated them very well."

00:16:27.490 --> 00:16:30.540
The problem, in her view, arose
from changes in land tenure,

00:16:30.540 --> 00:16:34.120
that it allowed, Le Noir, by
which she was referring to

00:16:34.120 --> 00:16:36.530
both the descendants of
enslaved West Africans

00:16:36.530 --> 00:16:39.540
and Indigenous Black, or Arab Berbers,

00:16:39.540 --> 00:16:43.043
access to property formerly
controlled by, Le Blanc,

00:16:44.900 --> 00:16:47.600
which would be non-Black Berbers.

00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:49.090
In addition, because descendants

00:16:49.090 --> 00:16:51.140
of enslaved and Black Indigenous people

00:16:51.140 --> 00:16:53.240
were now considered
members of tribal groups,

00:16:53.240 --> 00:16:55.580
they were able to sit on local councils

00:16:55.580 --> 00:16:58.430
and influence decisions about developing,

00:16:58.430 --> 00:17:00.430
or selling off communal land.

00:17:00.430 --> 00:17:02.630
She traced a square on
the table with her fingers

00:17:02.630 --> 00:17:05.827
and added, "Now that African
migrants are arriving

00:17:05.827 --> 00:17:10.367
"in the region, the Amazigh are
being invaded on all sides."

00:17:13.388 --> 00:17:16.270
There are many things that we
can unpack about this vignette

00:17:16.270 --> 00:17:20.430
that speak to the specific
character of racial politics

00:17:20.430 --> 00:17:23.880
in Morocco, including
questions of indigeneity,

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:28.700
race, descent, property
regimes, kinship and filiation.

00:17:28.700 --> 00:17:32.150
But what really struck me about
these very local struggles

00:17:32.150 --> 00:17:35.970
with decades, and in some
cases, centuries of...

00:17:35.970 --> 00:17:39.480
Built on centuries-old
categories of social difference

00:17:39.480 --> 00:17:42.240
and corresponding power
and resource inequalities,

00:17:42.240 --> 00:17:44.110
was that they were being interpolated

00:17:44.110 --> 00:17:48.880
into this very recent
transnational Euro-African border.

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:51.580
So, what I'm gonna spend the
rest of our time on today

00:17:51.580 --> 00:17:54.690
is showing how the border
is producing the social,

00:17:54.690 --> 00:17:57.080
especially in regards to race,

00:17:57.080 --> 00:18:00.210
in places far from the walls and fences

00:18:00.210 --> 00:18:02.610
and how the politics
of belonging in Morocco

00:18:02.610 --> 00:18:07.570
is increasingly being articulated
in the register of race.

00:18:07.570 --> 00:18:10.030
And I wanna touch briefly
on how outsourcing,

00:18:10.030 --> 00:18:12.590
or externalizing border enforcement

00:18:12.590 --> 00:18:14.490
from Europe to its neighbors,

00:18:14.490 --> 00:18:18.810
can enable the concealment
of European culpability,

00:18:18.810 --> 00:18:22.540
but it also can offer a way
to trace the border's logics,

00:18:22.540 --> 00:18:26.440
and operations across vast spaces.

00:18:26.440 --> 00:18:28.620
Next, I'll discuss
Morocco's racial history

00:18:28.620 --> 00:18:31.760
in contemporary context in
which the border intervenes.

00:18:31.760 --> 00:18:35.180
And then finally, while
Blackness is consolidated

00:18:35.180 --> 00:18:37.040
as a signifier of otherness,

00:18:37.040 --> 00:18:41.570
I propose that it has facilitated,
or deepened mobilizations

00:18:41.570 --> 00:18:43.950
against white supremacy throughout

00:18:43.950 --> 00:18:47.603
the Euro-African border.
(pages rustling)

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:52.900
Teresa, I'm assuming you will intervene

00:18:52.900 --> 00:18:54.130
if there's something going wrong,

00:18:54.130 --> 00:18:56.970
so I'm just gonna keep
going 'cause all I see

00:18:56.970 --> 00:18:58.433
is the PowerPoint.

00:19:01.550 --> 00:19:03.630
I just found this map and
I think it's wonderful

00:19:03.630 --> 00:19:07.693
because it gives you a sense
of this externalized border.

00:19:08.570 --> 00:19:10.590
According to this map,
the blue dotted line,

00:19:10.590 --> 00:19:12.900
which goes through the Mediterranean,

00:19:12.900 --> 00:19:16.850
is sort of where the
European border is imagined.

00:19:16.850 --> 00:19:19.320
I'm not exactly sure why
it runs through Turkey

00:19:19.320 --> 00:19:20.280
in this case, but

00:19:21.140 --> 00:19:23.180
maybe we can talk about that.
(Leslie chuckles)

00:19:23.180 --> 00:19:24.900
But then you have the neighbors,

00:19:24.900 --> 00:19:27.340
you have North Africa who,

00:19:27.340 --> 00:19:29.420
North African countries who participate

00:19:29.420 --> 00:19:32.820
in containing and preventing migrants from

00:19:33.990 --> 00:19:36.410
their own people and then
also from other places,

00:19:36.410 --> 00:19:39.020
from reaching, or crossing that border.

00:19:39.020 --> 00:19:42.100
And then I think what this map also shows

00:19:42.100 --> 00:19:43.340
is that the externalized border

00:19:43.340 --> 00:19:45.130
is moving further and further south

00:19:45.130 --> 00:19:48.050
through various other kinds
of research partnerships,

00:19:48.050 --> 00:19:51.080
so that the whole journey
from, say for example,

00:19:51.080 --> 00:19:56.080
Cameroon to Libya, or to
Morocco is heavily bordered

00:19:56.440 --> 00:20:00.190
and is overlaid with various agreements

00:20:00.190 --> 00:20:03.923
between states and
contractors and other people.

00:20:05.450 --> 00:20:07.720
So, my colleague, Lorena Gazzotti and I

00:20:07.720 --> 00:20:11.210
recently authored a piece that
subjects analytical concepts

00:20:11.210 --> 00:20:13.320
common to border and migration studies,

00:20:13.320 --> 00:20:15.580
to post-colonial critique.

00:20:15.580 --> 00:20:19.210
We argue that uncritical
use of externalization

00:20:19.210 --> 00:20:22.630
runs the risk of reproducing
colonial narratives

00:20:22.630 --> 00:20:26.150
of Europe as the agent of history.

00:20:26.150 --> 00:20:27.960
Following (indiscernible) in Syria,

00:20:27.960 --> 00:20:30.460
we argue that externalizations presentism

00:20:30.460 --> 00:20:35.280
the idea that it's unique and
also that migration is new,

00:20:35.280 --> 00:20:38.640
or is a crisis, is ahistorical.

00:20:38.640 --> 00:20:41.860
European powers have been
subverting African autonomy

00:20:41.860 --> 00:20:45.430
for centuries as Martin
Lumbar Peterson has argued,

00:20:45.430 --> 00:20:49.080
and imposing various mobility
regimes that facilitate,

00:20:49.080 --> 00:20:54.080
or suppress the movement
of precarious people.

00:20:54.150 --> 00:20:56.940
Nevertheless, externalization
also enables us

00:20:56.940 --> 00:21:00.270
to move beyond uncritical
analyses of border enforcement

00:21:00.270 --> 00:21:02.670
that center individual states.

00:21:02.670 --> 00:21:05.370
So, it allows us to
trace relations of power

00:21:05.370 --> 00:21:07.250
in circuits of capital that structure

00:21:07.250 --> 00:21:11.580
the transnational, heterogeneous
Euro-African border.

00:21:11.580 --> 00:21:13.570
When used critically and historically,

00:21:13.570 --> 00:21:16.310
externalization can help
us discern the connections

00:21:16.310 --> 00:21:20.020
that obtain across scales
and among different actors.

00:21:20.020 --> 00:21:21.940
By bringing such connections to the fore,

00:21:21.940 --> 00:21:24.940
we can track transnational
circulations of race

00:21:24.940 --> 00:21:28.530
and racist ideologies that
are central to the animation

00:21:28.530 --> 00:21:32.350
of local, specific racial
formations and histories

00:21:32.350 --> 00:21:34.200
in places like Morocco.

00:21:34.200 --> 00:21:36.610
So, I'm gonna talk a little
bit about these histories

00:21:36.610 --> 00:21:38.380
before turning to how they articulate

00:21:38.380 --> 00:21:40.963
with border externalization
in Morocco today.

00:21:43.716 --> 00:21:44.930
(object thudding)
Wonder if I can move this.

00:21:44.930 --> 00:21:45.763
I don't know if you guys,

00:21:45.763 --> 00:21:46.670
if that's in the way.

00:21:46.670 --> 00:21:48.390
Oh, well, I'll just leave it.

00:21:48.390 --> 00:21:50.580
For a long time there's been silence

00:21:50.580 --> 00:21:52.730
around the subject of race in Morocco,

00:21:52.730 --> 00:21:54.270
both in public conversations,

00:21:54.270 --> 00:21:55.950
but also in academic scholarship

00:21:55.950 --> 00:21:59.740
where it's often folded into
discussions of ethnicity.

00:21:59.740 --> 00:22:02.830
Despite this silence,
race and racialization

00:22:02.830 --> 00:22:06.320
permeate Moroccan and Maghrebi societies.

00:22:06.320 --> 00:22:09.570
Chouki El Hamel points to the
pre-colonial and Islamic roots

00:22:09.570 --> 00:22:11.340
or the Black, white binary,

00:22:11.340 --> 00:22:14.520
suggesting that perhaps
the Islamic Mediterranean

00:22:14.520 --> 00:22:17.120
influenced, or shaped
the racial hierarchies

00:22:17.980 --> 00:22:20.790
that became entrenched in the Americas.

00:22:20.790 --> 00:22:23.400
The Trans-Saharan slave trade reverberates

00:22:23.400 --> 00:22:26.200
in Moroccan social relations as evidenced

00:22:27.110 --> 00:22:30.230
by the use of the word, al abd, slave,

00:22:30.230 --> 00:22:32.490
to refer to a dark-skinned Moroccan

00:22:32.490 --> 00:22:35.780
and the persistent idea that
phenotypically Black Berbers

00:22:35.780 --> 00:22:38.330
are not actually Indigenous.

00:22:38.330 --> 00:22:41.330
The colonial project in
Morocco and across Africa

00:22:41.330 --> 00:22:44.810
has linked whiteness to
superiority and modernity

00:22:44.810 --> 00:22:47.590
and Blackness to tradition, prehistory

00:22:47.590 --> 00:22:49.440
and under-development.

00:22:49.440 --> 00:22:52.230
Today, the accretion of
anti-Blackness is discernible

00:22:52.230 --> 00:22:54.960
in myriad ways that
include racial profiling

00:22:54.960 --> 00:22:58.820
by security agents, the
casual use of racist language

00:22:58.820 --> 00:23:01.900
by ordinary Moroccans and
the frequent appearance

00:23:01.900 --> 00:23:04.150
of blackface and racial stereotypes

00:23:04.150 --> 00:23:05.990
in entertainment and media.

00:23:05.990 --> 00:23:07.700
And you can see an example here.

00:23:07.700 --> 00:23:10.880
This was a really famous
example that came out recently

00:23:10.880 --> 00:23:13.040
because Mariam Hussein here,

00:23:13.040 --> 00:23:16.890
is expressing her solidarity
with U.S. protests,

00:23:16.890 --> 00:23:19.770
the Black Lives Matter
protests in the United States,

00:23:19.770 --> 00:23:22.200
in the wake of the murder of George Floyd.

00:23:22.200 --> 00:23:24.510
And the way that she
decided to do this was

00:23:25.796 --> 00:23:26.843
to wear blackface.

00:23:29.100 --> 00:23:31.790
So, as anthropologist,
Laura Menin has argued,

00:23:31.790 --> 00:23:33.810
racial formations and racism

00:23:33.810 --> 00:23:36.700
are local particular and contextual.

00:23:36.700 --> 00:23:39.670
This is a common position
among both historians

00:23:39.670 --> 00:23:42.320
and anthropologists working
on questions of difference

00:23:42.320 --> 00:23:45.500
in the Maghreb and the
African continent as a whole.

00:23:45.500 --> 00:23:47.430
What's important about this perspective

00:23:47.430 --> 00:23:50.070
is that it safeguards
against imposing analyses,

00:23:50.070 --> 00:23:54.003
or theories derived from other
contexts to the Moroccan one.

00:23:55.060 --> 00:23:56.970
But going back to the
point made at the beginning

00:23:56.970 --> 00:24:00.330
about scale, context is never only local.

00:24:00.330 --> 00:24:03.940
And indeed the local is
always bound up in relations

00:24:03.940 --> 00:24:07.310
that extend well beyond
the particular location.

00:24:07.310 --> 00:24:11.060
I think Fatima Zahra's story
gets at both this relationality

00:24:11.060 --> 00:24:12.630
and its contingency.

00:24:12.630 --> 00:24:15.910
Morocco is not a blank slate
when it comes to race relations

00:24:15.910 --> 00:24:18.930
or racial politics, but
neither is it closed off

00:24:18.930 --> 00:24:22.820
from the past or present
global racial ideologies

00:24:22.820 --> 00:24:24.600
and geopolitics.

00:24:24.600 --> 00:24:27.540
Tracing the border as it
connects as well as separates

00:24:27.540 --> 00:24:29.610
Africa and Europe,
makes it possible to see

00:24:29.610 --> 00:24:33.110
how race circulates, is
transnationally constituted

00:24:33.110 --> 00:24:34.620
and bound up in systems of power

00:24:34.620 --> 00:24:37.320
that cut across local and international,

00:24:37.320 --> 00:24:41.280
reworking, intensifying,
unsettling and refusing meanings

00:24:41.280 --> 00:24:42.683
in various places.

00:24:44.200 --> 00:24:47.230
So again, Europe's outsourced
border enforcement program

00:24:47.230 --> 00:24:50.770
and the ensemble that now
comprises the Euro-African border

00:24:50.770 --> 00:24:53.210
is impacting, not only
notions of Blackness,

00:24:53.210 --> 00:24:57.160
but reinforcing hegemonic
conceptions of whiteness

00:24:57.160 --> 00:25:00.020
across Moroccan social space.

00:25:00.020 --> 00:25:03.060
Blackness, which has long been
a signifier of inferiority

00:25:03.060 --> 00:25:06.380
in Morocco, has become
consolidated with illegality,

00:25:06.380 --> 00:25:09.820
with riskiness and with out-of-placeness,

00:25:09.820 --> 00:25:13.180
sticking to Yusira Ahmed's
term, to other people

00:25:13.180 --> 00:25:16.910
who are not explicitly the
targets of border violence.

00:25:16.910 --> 00:25:19.310
So, this is a case with Timothy Hucks.

00:25:19.310 --> 00:25:21.440
Timothy Hucks was a U.S.
English teacher who,

00:25:21.440 --> 00:25:24.350
while living in Rabat,
was rounded up by police

00:25:24.350 --> 00:25:27.360
and internally deported to
the south of the country.

00:25:27.360 --> 00:25:29.790
He had stepped out of his
house to run a quick errand

00:25:29.790 --> 00:25:31.780
and left his passport at home.

00:25:31.780 --> 00:25:34.350
Police did not believe
that he as a Black man,

00:25:34.350 --> 00:25:35.480
was a U.S. citizen

00:25:35.480 --> 00:25:38.170
and thought he was an
undocumented migrant.

00:25:38.170 --> 00:25:41.360
Lorena Gazzotti has written
about how in Morocco,

00:25:41.360 --> 00:25:44.260
Black people are routinely
policed and deported

00:25:44.260 --> 00:25:47.940
regardless of their legal
status, while at the same time,

00:25:47.940 --> 00:25:50.370
white Europeans who overstay their visas,

00:25:50.370 --> 00:25:52.610
can expect few consequences,

00:25:52.610 --> 00:25:55.440
none of them violent, or immediate.

00:25:55.440 --> 00:25:57.600
While Hucks eventually returned to Rabat

00:25:57.600 --> 00:26:00.660
and then to the United States,
and I think his citizenship

00:26:00.660 --> 00:26:04.283
had something to do with
his ability to move,

00:26:06.640 --> 00:26:09.830
Alain Toussaint, another
teacher and legal resident,

00:26:09.830 --> 00:26:12.320
originally from the
Democratic Republic of Congo,

00:26:12.320 --> 00:26:15.100
was similarly rounded up and died

00:26:15.100 --> 00:26:16.810
after being thrown from a moving bus

00:26:16.810 --> 00:26:18.823
during an internal deportation.

00:26:20.105 --> 00:26:22.160
(pages rustling)

00:26:22.160 --> 00:26:23.490
So, oh, sorry.

00:26:23.490 --> 00:26:26.330
So, the conflation of
Blackness with out-of-placeness

00:26:26.330 --> 00:26:29.100
attaches to Black Moroccans as well.

00:26:29.100 --> 00:26:32.050
During field work, Black
Moroccans recounted examples

00:26:32.050 --> 00:26:35.240
of how other Moroccans
would mutter racial epithets

00:26:35.240 --> 00:26:38.330
under their breath, like
at the hanut or market,

00:26:38.330 --> 00:26:41.750
assuming that the person didn't
understand Moroccan Arabic.

00:26:41.750 --> 00:26:44.713
And in a poignant example
that a friend told me about,

00:26:46.791 --> 00:26:49.380
in 2017, he was watching a sports program

00:26:49.380 --> 00:26:51.750
and a Moroccan TV journalist
was on the streets

00:26:51.750 --> 00:26:53.560
in Casablanca interviewing people

00:26:53.560 --> 00:26:56.500
about an upcoming soccer match.

00:26:56.500 --> 00:26:58.580
When the journalist
encountered a Black Moroccan,

00:26:58.580 --> 00:27:02.100
he switched to French assuming
the man was a foreigner.

00:27:02.100 --> 00:27:04.130
The man replied in Moroccan Arabic.

00:27:04.130 --> 00:27:06.043
They went back and forth like this

00:27:06.043 --> 00:27:08.610
with the interviewer not
believing the man was Moroccan

00:27:08.610 --> 00:27:11.803
until he finally said in exasperation,

00:27:11.803 --> 00:27:14.310
"(speaking in foreign
language)," long live the king.

00:27:14.310 --> 00:27:17.310
In contrast, whiteness
has gained more salience

00:27:17.310 --> 00:27:20.400
as a way to assert one's
authorization in a space

00:27:20.400 --> 00:27:23.100
at the national scale,
but also at other scales.

00:27:23.100 --> 00:27:26.040
So, like Fatima Zahra, many
Moroccans that I spoke to

00:27:26.040 --> 00:27:30.310
would probably refer to their
identity as white in ref...

00:27:30.310 --> 00:27:31.510
I'm sorry,

00:27:31.510 --> 00:27:35.220
Their identity as Muslim, Arab,
or Berber, perhaps Jewish,

00:27:35.220 --> 00:27:38.410
or, more commonly, just Moroccan,

00:27:38.410 --> 00:27:41.980
but referred to themselves as
white in reference to Black,

00:27:41.980 --> 00:27:44.050
or African migrants.

00:27:44.050 --> 00:27:47.260
So, drawing a distinction between
so-called African migrants

00:27:47.260 --> 00:27:50.330
and themselves, helped
them to fix a boundary

00:27:50.330 --> 00:27:52.640
that aligns the Maghreb with Europe

00:27:52.640 --> 00:27:55.260
at the expense of producing Africa,

00:27:55.260 --> 00:27:58.913
using the language of European
apartheid as beyond the pale.

00:28:02.530 --> 00:28:06.540
The global pandemic has
exposed how harm is directed

00:28:06.540 --> 00:28:09.470
to racialized, gendered and othered people

00:28:09.470 --> 00:28:11.640
in society structured in dominance.

00:28:11.640 --> 00:28:14.120
The murder of George
Floyd and Breonna Taylor,

00:28:14.120 --> 00:28:17.190
in my country, has
provoked worldwide protests

00:28:17.190 --> 00:28:20.120
in solidarity with Black U.S. citizens,

00:28:20.120 --> 00:28:23.550
but it's also prompted
testimonies from Black Arabs

00:28:23.550 --> 00:28:26.160
and other Black Middle Eastern
and North African people

00:28:26.160 --> 00:28:29.090
about the pervasive
discrimination that exists

00:28:29.090 --> 00:28:32.810
both in the diaspora and
in their home countries.

00:28:32.810 --> 00:28:34.770
Significantly, many of them are naming

00:28:34.770 --> 00:28:37.560
the marginalization
violence they experience

00:28:37.560 --> 00:28:40.490
as anti-Blackness and
expressing solidarity

00:28:40.490 --> 00:28:43.970
with Black migrants in
other African diasporas.

00:28:43.970 --> 00:28:46.850
So, these are two social media campaigns

00:28:46.850 --> 00:28:49.100
that I wanna just talk
about for a few minutes.

00:28:49.100 --> 00:28:50.610
There are many of them

00:28:50.610 --> 00:28:52.773
that have proliferated in recent years.

00:28:54.250 --> 00:28:56.043
Or in recent months, I should say.

00:28:57.119 --> 00:29:00.280
So, qahwa.nessness and Black Moroccans

00:29:01.170 --> 00:29:04.570
are providing platforms for
Black-identifying Moroccans

00:29:04.570 --> 00:29:07.410
and members of the Moroccan diaspora.

00:29:07.410 --> 00:29:09.080
Black Moroccans, as far as I can tell,

00:29:09.080 --> 00:29:11.510
is also affiliated with
activists and artists

00:29:11.510 --> 00:29:16.510
who were involved in the 2014
campaign, "Masmiytich Azzi,"

00:29:16.710 --> 00:29:18.903
which is, "My name is not Azzi,"

00:29:20.150 --> 00:29:24.660
Azzi connoting a very pejorative
word for a Black person.

00:29:24.660 --> 00:29:26.970
So, both of these Instagram accounts,

00:29:26.970 --> 00:29:30.370
like other accounts that
focus on the Gulf, or Iran,

00:29:30.370 --> 00:29:32.170
or the Arab world as a whole,

00:29:32.170 --> 00:29:34.270
are comprised of a mix of historical

00:29:34.270 --> 00:29:37.110
and contemporary images
of Blackness in Morocco,

00:29:37.110 --> 00:29:41.480
as well as testimonies of Black
and Afro-identified people

00:29:41.480 --> 00:29:44.380
speaking to their experiences
with anti-Blackness

00:29:44.380 --> 00:29:45.927
in their home countries.

00:29:45.927 --> 00:29:47.440
And this is one example.

00:29:47.440 --> 00:29:50.660
I think that this is the
organizer of qahwa.halfhalf,

00:29:50.660 --> 00:29:53.253
but I haven't gotten in
touch yet to confirm that.

00:29:54.900 --> 00:29:57.630
So, many of these testimonies
explicitly point out

00:29:57.630 --> 00:29:59.680
that racism is not just
something that happens

00:29:59.680 --> 00:30:01.180
in the United States or in Europe,

00:30:01.180 --> 00:30:05.053
but it's something that's also
woven into Moroccan society.

00:30:06.270 --> 00:30:08.870
And these Instagram
accounts provide a platform

00:30:08.870 --> 00:30:11.790
where Moroccan nationals and
Europeans of Moroccan descent

00:30:11.790 --> 00:30:15.330
reject the pressures to
orient toward whiteness,

00:30:15.330 --> 00:30:17.730
instead embracing
Blackness and Africanity,

00:30:17.730 --> 00:30:19.143
along with Moroccaness,

00:30:20.967 --> 00:30:23.860
Europeanness, Indigeneity, or Arabness.

00:30:23.860 --> 00:30:26.140
They write and speak about
identity and sociality

00:30:26.140 --> 00:30:27.500
in ways that destabilize,

00:30:27.500 --> 00:30:31.080
not only the Manichaeanisms
of Black, white binaries,

00:30:31.080 --> 00:30:32.840
but also monolithic ideas

00:30:32.840 --> 00:30:35.720
about racial identity and belonging.

00:30:35.720 --> 00:30:37.730
Their commentaries invoke the local

00:30:37.730 --> 00:30:40.920
and Moroccan specificity of
Blackness and anti-Blackness

00:30:40.920 --> 00:30:44.020
and how these are
entangled in transnational

00:30:44.020 --> 00:30:48.300
and transtemporal notions
of value and humanity.

00:30:48.300 --> 00:30:50.750
I think these are really exciting.

00:30:50.750 --> 00:30:51.780
I could go on a...

00:30:51.780 --> 00:30:53.350
I'm gonna wrap up really soon,

00:30:53.350 --> 00:30:55.500
but I can talk about
these for a long time.

00:30:56.500 --> 00:30:59.920
So, tracing the afterlives of slavery

00:30:59.920 --> 00:31:01.720
and European colonialism,

00:31:01.720 --> 00:31:04.930
they connect the condition
of the Haratin in Morocco

00:31:04.930 --> 00:31:07.780
and Mauritania to that
of la-bas in France.

00:31:07.780 --> 00:31:10.360
They interrogate the
role of anti-Blackness

00:31:10.360 --> 00:31:12.500
in Islamic history and jurisprudence.

00:31:12.500 --> 00:31:14.290
They protest foreign capitalists

00:31:14.290 --> 00:31:17.640
and militaristic interventions and so on.

00:31:17.640 --> 00:31:22.640
Their epistemological vision
is relational and social,

00:31:22.700 --> 00:31:25.890
simultaneously grounded
in place and specificity

00:31:25.890 --> 00:31:28.100
and mindful of the larger crosscurrents

00:31:28.100 --> 00:31:30.750
that we all get drawn into.

00:31:30.750 --> 00:31:33.960
Black Moroccans, with its
roots in immigrant solidarity

00:31:33.960 --> 00:31:37.720
and anti-racist activism and
the more recent qahwa.halfhalf,

00:31:37.720 --> 00:31:40.830
emerge from, I would
argue, (speaking French)

00:31:40.830 --> 00:31:43.530
shaped by the Euro-African
border's production

00:31:43.530 --> 00:31:45.770
of Blackness as otherness.

00:31:45.770 --> 00:31:49.010
The transnational project
of bordering Blackness

00:31:49.010 --> 00:31:52.490
has also enabled West and
Central African migrants,

00:31:52.490 --> 00:31:55.260
Black Moroccans, Moroccans in the diaspora

00:31:55.260 --> 00:31:59.370
experiencing anti-Islamic,
or anti-Black discrimination

00:31:59.370 --> 00:32:02.630
to assemble a transnational
African diaspora

00:32:02.630 --> 00:32:05.610
that refuses Blackness as negation,

00:32:05.610 --> 00:32:07.500
and instead understands Blackness

00:32:07.500 --> 00:32:11.580
as conditioning the possibility
for new internationalist

00:32:11.580 --> 00:32:13.763
and de-colonial features.

00:32:14.790 --> 00:32:17.363
I think that's the last example, yeah.

00:32:21.060 --> 00:32:21.980
Okay, so

00:32:23.220 --> 00:32:26.220
I think Teresa is gonna help me do this,

00:32:26.220 --> 00:32:30.960
but if you have questions,
you can put them in the chat.

00:32:30.960 --> 00:32:31.793
Or I don't know.

00:32:31.793 --> 00:32:33.890
What do you think, Teresa,
they can unmute themselves?

00:32:33.890 --> 00:32:35.460
- Yeah, they can unmute themselves.

00:32:35.460 --> 00:32:39.420
There was already one question
on the chat from Tasleem

00:32:39.420 --> 00:32:41.040
about the

00:32:42.400 --> 00:32:47.070
map that you show, so I
could think if you want to,

00:32:47.070 --> 00:32:51.590
if you want to show, ask
the question yourself?

00:32:51.590 --> 00:32:53.580
- Sure, okay, yeah.

00:32:53.580 --> 00:32:55.370
So, thanks for the question, Tasleem.

00:32:55.370 --> 00:32:57.677
So, you said, "Are the yellow bits

00:32:57.677 --> 00:33:00.517
"countries that are
partnering with North Africa

00:33:00.517 --> 00:33:02.517
"to protect the red border?"

00:33:03.370 --> 00:33:05.420
I think a lot of that...

00:33:07.420 --> 00:33:08.600
I guess I will go back to it.

00:33:08.600 --> 00:33:12.870
But I think that most of this,
because a lot of the funding

00:33:12.870 --> 00:33:15.870
is still driven by the European Union,

00:33:15.870 --> 00:33:18.240
these are partnerships that comprise,

00:33:18.240 --> 00:33:22.270
sort of multiple layers of partners

00:33:22.270 --> 00:33:25.990
that include, often Europeans,
North African states and

00:33:28.190 --> 00:33:31.120
states in this Sahara and
Western Africa, for example.

00:33:31.120 --> 00:33:33.720
So, I don't think it's
tiered in the sense that

00:33:34.610 --> 00:33:36.910
Morocco and Tunisia and Algeria and Libya

00:33:36.910 --> 00:33:40.010
cooperate with Europe and then,

00:33:40.010 --> 00:33:43.460
you know, Mali and Mauritania and Niger

00:33:43.460 --> 00:33:46.620
cooperate with Morocco and
Algeria, but instead it's,

00:33:46.620 --> 00:33:49.160
you know, it's these
multiple layers of programs

00:33:49.160 --> 00:33:51.333
that contribute to that across the board.

00:33:57.704 --> 00:34:00.287
And I see a question from Lydia

00:34:01.640 --> 00:34:04.490
that asks if there are
any Moroccan researchers

00:34:04.490 --> 00:34:08.620
working on these topics,
Blackness, externalized, borders,

00:34:08.620 --> 00:34:11.080
et cetera, in Moroccan universities.

00:34:11.080 --> 00:34:15.960
So, I think the answer to
that is yes, there are.

00:34:15.960 --> 00:34:17.810
Particularly working on the topic

00:34:17.810 --> 00:34:20.460
of the externalized border,
there are quite a few,

00:34:20.460 --> 00:34:22.823
both doing research,

00:34:24.130 --> 00:34:26.680
I mean, affiliated with
institutions in Europe

00:34:26.680 --> 00:34:29.500
and North America, but also
working within Morocco.

00:34:29.500 --> 00:34:32.820
When I first came to do research in 2017,

00:34:32.820 --> 00:34:36.460
I met with three researchers from Meknes

00:34:36.460 --> 00:34:39.490
who were doing phenomenal
work on the border

00:34:39.490 --> 00:34:42.003
and on migrant experiences there.

00:34:43.380 --> 00:34:45.233
And I am...

00:34:45.233 --> 00:34:46.580
(indiscernible) is the last name.

00:34:46.580 --> 00:34:48.550
I'm blanking temporarily on their names,

00:34:48.550 --> 00:34:50.760
but I can pass them along.

00:34:50.760 --> 00:34:54.220
In addition, my colleague
and I, Lorena and I,

00:34:54.220 --> 00:34:56.190
just co-edited a volume

00:34:56.190 --> 00:34:58.300
for the Journal of North African Studies

00:34:58.300 --> 00:35:00.980
that came out online this summer,

00:35:00.980 --> 00:35:03.460
that features a few people,

00:35:03.460 --> 00:35:07.000
a few researchers, from the
region and actually one migrant

00:35:08.740 --> 00:35:10.847
who is speaking about
the externalized border

00:35:10.847 --> 00:35:12.890
and their experience in Morocco.

00:35:12.890 --> 00:35:16.220
So, yeah, so that's some of that.

00:35:16.220 --> 00:35:20.470
In terms of Blackness, I do
think that there are some people

00:35:20.470 --> 00:35:22.790
from the North African diaspora

00:35:22.790 --> 00:35:24.990
who are starting to ask these questions.

00:35:24.990 --> 00:35:26.230
University of Pennsylvania,

00:35:26.230 --> 00:35:28.550
their Middle East Studies Department there

00:35:28.550 --> 00:35:30.513
has had some really interesting work.

00:35:32.459 --> 00:35:33.292
I'm really bad with names.

00:35:33.292 --> 00:35:36.660
Afifa Latifi, I believe is

00:35:38.400 --> 00:35:41.650
I think from Tunisia and is doing that,

00:35:41.650 --> 00:35:43.430
is thinking about these questions

00:35:45.514 --> 00:35:48.003
from a historical point, perspective.

00:35:49.390 --> 00:35:51.843
And yes, of course,
(indiscernible) points out as well.

00:35:51.843 --> 00:35:55.633
Said Sadiqi has been talking
about this for a long time.

00:35:56.530 --> 00:35:58.860
And then of course, in terms of Blackness,

00:35:58.860 --> 00:36:01.460
Chouki El Hamel literally wrote the book

00:36:01.460 --> 00:36:03.010
on Blackness in Morocco.

00:36:03.010 --> 00:36:06.163
And that book has recently
been translated into Arabic.

00:36:13.220 --> 00:36:15.790
Okay, Zeenab asked,

00:36:15.790 --> 00:36:19.620
what did I mean by
relational epistemologies?

00:36:19.620 --> 00:36:23.063
And this is when I was talking
about the Instagrammers.

00:36:24.490 --> 00:36:28.060
I think the emphasis that I'm
trying to make here is that

00:36:28.060 --> 00:36:31.810
these phenomena are neither
local, completely local,

00:36:31.810 --> 00:36:32.970
nor completely

00:36:34.936 --> 00:36:37.140
de-territorialized, or global only,

00:36:37.140 --> 00:36:40.050
but they are constantly
in relation to each other.

00:36:40.050 --> 00:36:42.100
And that also, the way that

00:36:43.080 --> 00:36:45.220
these protests are seeing them themselves

00:36:45.220 --> 00:36:48.600
is not in a comparative
sense where they're trying to

00:36:49.890 --> 00:36:52.430
show that anti-Blackness
in Morocco, for example,

00:36:52.430 --> 00:36:55.640
is commensurate with maybe anti-Arab,

00:36:55.640 --> 00:37:00.270
or anti-Islamic racism in
France, but trying to constitute

00:37:00.270 --> 00:37:01.683
or organized,

00:37:04.430 --> 00:37:07.420
organize solidarities that recognize

00:37:07.420 --> 00:37:09.760
that a lot of this has to
do with white supremacy,

00:37:09.760 --> 00:37:11.630
it has to do with capitalist development

00:37:11.630 --> 00:37:13.880
and even development in the world.

00:37:13.880 --> 00:37:15.330
And it has to do with

00:37:15.330 --> 00:37:18.373
long histories of colonial
intervention as well.

00:37:28.200 --> 00:37:30.450
Any other questions?

00:37:30.450 --> 00:37:34.493
I'm also really happy to
hear comments from folks too.

00:37:35.960 --> 00:37:37.823
'Cause I imagine you have opinions.

00:37:39.864 --> 00:37:42.531
(Leslie laughs)

00:37:43.610 --> 00:37:46.097
Okay, so Iman said,

00:37:46.097 --> 00:37:47.837
"I was wondering if you
could talk a little more

00:37:47.837 --> 00:37:51.777
"about how the presence of
Sub-Saharan Africans in Morocco

00:37:51.777 --> 00:37:54.507
"conceals the fact that many of the people

00:37:54.507 --> 00:37:56.347
"migrating to Europe from Morocco

00:37:56.347 --> 00:37:58.627
"are actually Moroccan nationals

00:37:58.627 --> 00:38:02.307
"and how that affects the
Morocco EU relationship?"

00:38:03.190 --> 00:38:04.360
Thanks for that question.

00:38:04.360 --> 00:38:06.660
These are really great
questions, all of them.

00:38:09.480 --> 00:38:12.290
Yeah, so I think...

00:38:12.290 --> 00:38:13.740
This is something that I talk about

00:38:13.740 --> 00:38:17.070
in some of my other work, but
the way that I think about it

00:38:17.070 --> 00:38:19.810
is that in Morocco's participation

00:38:21.370 --> 00:38:24.630
in the externalized border is often framed

00:38:24.630 --> 00:38:27.310
as containing, or preventing the mobility

00:38:27.310 --> 00:38:30.210
of people from Sub-Saharan Africa.

00:38:30.210 --> 00:38:33.870
And as many of us know, Moroccan nationals

00:38:33.870 --> 00:38:37.620
constitute the largest non,

00:38:37.620 --> 00:38:42.620
largest non-national population in Europe,

00:38:42.850 --> 00:38:45.310
many of whom, of course,
arrived by legal means,

00:38:45.310 --> 00:38:46.423
but not all.

00:38:47.300 --> 00:38:48.930
But it's reworking.

00:38:48.930 --> 00:38:51.020
When I first moved to Morocco in 2001,

00:38:51.020 --> 00:38:52.580
there was a lot of discussion,

00:38:52.580 --> 00:38:55.930
and you see this in literature as well,

00:38:55.930 --> 00:38:58.100
a lot of discussion
about Moroccan migrants,

00:38:58.100 --> 00:39:01.330
or Moroccan immigrants who
are trying to get to Europe.

00:39:01.330 --> 00:39:04.880
And I feel like that discourse has shifted

00:39:04.880 --> 00:39:07.540
despite the fact that
really the numbers of people

00:39:07.540 --> 00:39:10.850
moving from Morocco to Europe,
from West or Central Africa

00:39:10.850 --> 00:39:13.260
are still quite small.

00:39:13.260 --> 00:39:16.023
So, I think it reframes,

00:39:17.171 --> 00:39:20.350
for Morocco it reframes
the problem of migration

00:39:20.350 --> 00:39:22.650
if migration is at all a problem,

00:39:22.650 --> 00:39:25.240
as something that is from outside,

00:39:25.240 --> 00:39:26.840
that is from West or Central Africa,

00:39:26.840 --> 00:39:28.533
rather than something that is,

00:39:29.490 --> 00:39:32.580
that is related to Moroccan
nationals themselves,

00:39:32.580 --> 00:39:33.913
if that makes sense.

00:39:35.790 --> 00:39:37.780
And of course, the EU is also

00:39:39.566 --> 00:39:43.580
addressing the issue of
Moroccan national migration.

00:39:43.580 --> 00:39:45.250
But one of the things that they do

00:39:45.250 --> 00:39:47.770
in exchange for Morocco's cooperation,

00:39:47.770 --> 00:39:52.770
is offer more short-term visas
for agricultural laborers

00:39:53.030 --> 00:39:55.330
and people like that,
as well as student visas

00:39:56.420 --> 00:39:57.930
and facilitate some of the ways

00:39:57.930 --> 00:40:00.430
for people to go work in Europe,

00:40:00.430 --> 00:40:03.400
or join family in Europe more easily.

00:40:03.400 --> 00:40:05.470
But some of that is, at least in theory,

00:40:05.470 --> 00:40:08.210
contingent upon Morocco's cooperation

00:40:08.210 --> 00:40:10.970
in keeping West and Central Africans out,

00:40:10.970 --> 00:40:13.020
or keeping them from crossing the border.

00:40:14.290 --> 00:40:15.640
Does that make sense, Iman?

00:40:16.930 --> 00:40:18.453
- [Iman] Yeah, thank you so much.

00:40:22.110 --> 00:40:23.520
- This is Tasleem.

00:40:23.520 --> 00:40:26.790
I wanted to ask you if
you see parallels in

00:40:27.730 --> 00:40:31.400
this situation here

00:40:31.400 --> 00:40:34.273
relative to the U.S.

00:40:35.310 --> 00:40:39.810
South American, like
we have this caravan...

00:40:39.810 --> 00:40:42.063
I'm just gonna use the
words even here are used.

00:40:42.063 --> 00:40:45.830
So, the caravan coming through and then

00:40:45.830 --> 00:40:48.430
the U.S. trying to partner with Mexico

00:40:51.122 --> 00:40:52.230
to make that border further.

00:40:52.230 --> 00:40:56.020
I mean, it kinda reminded me
of this whole situation here

00:40:56.020 --> 00:40:58.930
when you described the Moroccan situation.

00:40:58.930 --> 00:41:03.183
I'm wondering what are the
parallels and differences?

00:41:04.200 --> 00:41:05.500
- Yeah, that's a great question.

00:41:05.500 --> 00:41:07.350
I think there's a lot.
(Leslie chuckles)

00:41:07.350 --> 00:41:09.440
I think there are a lot of parallels.

00:41:09.440 --> 00:41:13.260
And now I'm gonna just make
things up, but I feel like

00:41:13.260 --> 00:41:17.350
the EU sort of pioneered this
and the U.S. jumped on-board,

00:41:17.350 --> 00:41:19.440
but I could be wrong about that.

00:41:19.440 --> 00:41:23.930
But I do know from people
doing research in Mexico,

00:41:23.930 --> 00:41:28.250
that there's increasing numbers
of Central American migrants

00:41:28.250 --> 00:41:31.780
who are stuck in Mexico and
the U.S. is pushing them back.

00:41:31.780 --> 00:41:32.970
Instead of...

00:41:32.970 --> 00:41:35.790
They're trying to intercept
them before they cross,

00:41:35.790 --> 00:41:37.420
or right as they cross the border

00:41:37.420 --> 00:41:39.160
so that they don't have to deport them.

00:41:39.160 --> 00:41:40.630
They can push them back into Mexico

00:41:40.630 --> 00:41:42.840
and then it becomes Mexico's problem.

00:41:42.840 --> 00:41:45.090
And the conditions for Central Americans

00:41:45.090 --> 00:41:46.630
has gotten much worse.

00:41:46.630 --> 00:41:49.793
And my understanding from, you now,

00:41:50.770 --> 00:41:53.740
from folks, either doing
research in Mexico,

00:41:53.740 --> 00:41:56.870
or actually just from Mexicans
that are living there,

00:41:56.870 --> 00:42:00.329
is that discrimination

00:42:00.329 --> 00:42:02.780
and sort of anti-Indigenous

00:42:03.630 --> 00:42:07.780
racism against Central
Americans, has become a huge...

00:42:10.183 --> 00:42:12.210
Has become significant in the way that

00:42:12.210 --> 00:42:16.290
the Central American
migration is characterized now

00:42:16.290 --> 00:42:17.213
in Mexico.

00:42:19.620 --> 00:42:21.650
And I think the U.S. I don't know...

00:42:21.650 --> 00:42:24.260
I know that the U.S. funds some of that,

00:42:24.260 --> 00:42:25.970
just the way that the EU funds it,

00:42:25.970 --> 00:42:29.498
but Donald Trump apparently
doesn't know that, so.

00:42:29.498 --> 00:42:31.590
(Both chuckle)

00:42:31.590 --> 00:42:33.060
- Because that also the...

00:42:34.320 --> 00:42:35.990
I think you called it the frontier zone,

00:42:35.990 --> 00:42:37.980
it's starting to feel like that too,

00:42:37.980 --> 00:42:42.120
on that border on the
Mexican side of the border

00:42:42.120 --> 00:42:44.980
of that space where--

00:42:44.980 --> 00:42:46.680
- Yeah.
- people who are stuck

00:42:46.680 --> 00:42:49.500
or setting up.
- Completely

00:42:49.500 --> 00:42:51.240
and now that asylum claims

00:42:51.240 --> 00:42:55.350
are either not being processed,
or processed so slowly,

00:42:55.350 --> 00:42:56.970
that there's just an...

00:42:56.970 --> 00:42:59.690
More and more people are
amassing on that border.

00:42:59.690 --> 00:43:01.050
And then of course, you know,

00:43:01.050 --> 00:43:03.420
the United States has a
hundred mile buffer zone

00:43:03.420 --> 00:43:05.840
around all of its borders.

00:43:05.840 --> 00:43:08.620
So, that's another thing.

00:43:08.620 --> 00:43:10.910
And it can act with a
certain level of impunity

00:43:10.910 --> 00:43:15.093
that doesn't apply in other
places in American territory.

00:43:17.300 --> 00:43:18.133
Yeah.

00:43:20.450 --> 00:43:24.430
- Hi, thank you so much,
Leslie, for your talk.

00:43:24.430 --> 00:43:27.610
I was curious if you
can speak to the sort of

00:43:27.610 --> 00:43:32.480
humanitarian laws governing
these deportations

00:43:32.480 --> 00:43:34.003
because I am curious.

00:43:35.047 --> 00:43:36.823
I am thinking so much
about African migrants

00:43:36.823 --> 00:43:40.740
who are going into Italy and
you have these Italian boats

00:43:40.740 --> 00:43:41.850
that are supposed to save them

00:43:41.850 --> 00:43:43.800
and then you have these asylum laws

00:43:43.800 --> 00:43:46.300
and you have, of course,
the humanitarian laws

00:43:46.300 --> 00:43:47.830
that they can't, you know...

00:43:50.140 --> 00:43:53.754
I don't know, I think I
encountered this concept of

00:43:53.754 --> 00:43:54.730
(speaking foreign language)

00:43:54.730 --> 00:43:57.690
in thinking about the
South American border

00:43:57.690 --> 00:44:00.810
where you can't legally send
people back, who are in danger.

00:44:00.810 --> 00:44:03.380
So, is there any sort of....

00:44:03.380 --> 00:44:07.610
I mean, this partnership between the EU

00:44:07.610 --> 00:44:10.670
is, I guess, quite concerning
when thinking about the laws

00:44:10.670 --> 00:44:14.180
that also the EU and the UN uphold

00:44:14.180 --> 00:44:15.940
in terms of humanitarianism.

00:44:15.940 --> 00:44:18.023
- Yeah, that's a great question.

00:44:19.801 --> 00:44:20.634
(Leslie sighs)

00:44:20.634 --> 00:44:22.860
Well, I'll just say the first
thing that comes to my mind is

00:44:22.860 --> 00:44:27.190
I get so mad when people are
rescued in the Mediterranean

00:44:27.190 --> 00:44:29.150
by being sent back to Libya, right?

00:44:29.150 --> 00:44:31.290
Or I get Morocco.

00:44:31.290 --> 00:44:33.730
I'd Google where it says
Morocco and migrants

00:44:33.730 --> 00:44:38.730
and they'll say like
Moroccan coast guard rescued

00:44:39.260 --> 00:44:41.880
a certain amount of migrants
in the Mediterranean

00:44:42.780 --> 00:44:45.330
when really it's intercepting
and pushing them back,

00:44:45.330 --> 00:44:47.410
or bringing them back
to Moroccan territory.

00:44:47.410 --> 00:44:51.050
So, that discourses,
humanitarian discourses,

00:44:51.050 --> 00:44:52.470
get really enmeshed.

00:44:52.470 --> 00:44:56.190
And that isn't to say that there
isn't rescue also happening

00:44:56.190 --> 00:44:57.610
in the Mediterranean,

00:44:57.610 --> 00:45:01.550
but those discourses get
really enmeshed with security

00:45:01.550 --> 00:45:03.110
and with securitization.

00:45:03.110 --> 00:45:06.770
And so, it's a really
complicated situation.

00:45:06.770 --> 00:45:11.000
In 2014, Morocco, for which
they should be lauded,

00:45:11.000 --> 00:45:13.313
reformed their migration policy.

00:45:14.640 --> 00:45:16.540
Some of the things that
they did was to offer

00:45:16.540 --> 00:45:19.050
two periods of exceptional regularization,

00:45:19.050 --> 00:45:22.450
where people could actually
get residency permits

00:45:22.450 --> 00:45:26.373
for a year or more, a year
and it could be renewed.

00:45:28.320 --> 00:45:33.320
In addition they, at least on
paper, at least aspirationally

00:45:33.700 --> 00:45:37.490
would provide healthcare
and education for kids

00:45:37.490 --> 00:45:39.123
and for other people.

00:45:41.140 --> 00:45:43.030
I'm trying to think what
else off the top of my head.

00:45:43.030 --> 00:45:46.560
But those were pretty significant,
particularly if we think,

00:45:46.560 --> 00:45:48.550
if you compare them to

00:45:48.550 --> 00:45:50.380
the context in the
United States right now,

00:45:50.380 --> 00:45:51.880
it is something to be lauded.

00:45:51.880 --> 00:45:55.775
But what it did is kind of put people,

00:45:55.775 --> 00:45:58.420
it kept people from traveling
where they wanted to travel

00:45:58.420 --> 00:46:00.350
and in some cases, it just

00:46:01.910 --> 00:46:03.040
put them on hold

00:46:03.040 --> 00:46:05.583
because they were still
not able to get jobs,

00:46:06.620 --> 00:46:09.110
they were still experiencing
police violence,

00:46:09.110 --> 00:46:11.440
they were still at the
risk of being rounded up

00:46:11.440 --> 00:46:13.990
at any moment and being
mistaken for someone

00:46:13.990 --> 00:46:16.310
that didn't have residency papers.

00:46:16.310 --> 00:46:19.000
Also, there were limits in
terms of who got regularized

00:46:19.000 --> 00:46:21.700
and who didn't and how long
you had to have been there

00:46:21.700 --> 00:46:24.330
and whether you were
there at the right time.

00:46:24.330 --> 00:46:28.270
So, I think actually
probably a little bit more

00:46:28.270 --> 00:46:31.180
than in the United States-Mexico context,

00:46:31.180 --> 00:46:33.840
humanitarian language and
sort of liberal discourses

00:46:33.840 --> 00:46:36.810
about human rights get deployed a lot,

00:46:36.810 --> 00:46:38.590
but not necessarily...

00:46:40.870 --> 00:46:44.260
They're not inscribed
into law in the same way.

00:46:44.260 --> 00:46:45.093
And,

00:46:46.725 --> 00:46:48.689
you know, just to do a
little bit more press,

00:46:48.689 --> 00:46:51.150
(Leslie chuckles)
or shameless promotion,

00:46:51.150 --> 00:46:53.320
the Journal of North African Studies

00:46:54.620 --> 00:46:58.240
special issue that just came out on this

00:46:58.240 --> 00:47:00.870
has some articles that
specifically address that.

00:47:00.870 --> 00:47:04.140
And one of them is by Katharina
Natter and she talks about

00:47:06.508 --> 00:47:10.110
how the sort of liberal
discourse in humanitarianism

00:47:10.110 --> 00:47:13.190
on the Moroccan state enables
it to be authoritarian

00:47:13.190 --> 00:47:14.340
in other ways.

00:47:14.340 --> 00:47:15.900
So,

00:47:15.900 --> 00:47:18.730
we have Herach conflict,

00:47:18.730 --> 00:47:22.310
or repression, I guess you would say

00:47:22.310 --> 00:47:24.300
and we're seeing a lot more media

00:47:24.300 --> 00:47:27.820
experiencing oppression in Morocco.

00:47:27.820 --> 00:47:30.740
Women have been subjected to court cases

00:47:30.740 --> 00:47:33.860
related to their sexuality
and things like that.

00:47:33.860 --> 00:47:37.740
So, there is a way too, in
which this humanitarian language

00:47:37.740 --> 00:47:40.693
conceals other kinds of behavior.

00:47:41.840 --> 00:47:44.450
So, those are some of the
things that I'm thinking of

00:47:44.450 --> 00:47:45.520
off the top of my head.

00:47:45.520 --> 00:47:47.666
Does that answer your question, Faith?

00:47:47.666 --> 00:47:49.480
- Yes, thank you so much.

00:47:49.480 --> 00:47:50.483
- Okay, great.

00:47:51.980 --> 00:47:54.120
Jennifer, you said...

00:47:54.120 --> 00:47:56.680
Yes, "When you talk about
resistance to the fact

00:47:59.017 --> 00:48:00.363
"or regarding..."

00:48:02.190 --> 00:48:03.640
Jennifer, can you tell me what you said?

00:48:03.640 --> 00:48:04.750
I missed the first part.

00:48:04.750 --> 00:48:07.200
Can you clarify?

00:48:07.200 --> 00:48:08.883
- [Jennifer] Sorry, I had
(indiscernible) deleted.

00:48:10.741 --> 00:48:11.574
- No, it's okay.
- I'm--

00:48:11.574 --> 00:48:14.410
- I do that all the time.
- I was just (sound cuts out)

00:48:14.410 --> 00:48:16.560
how you talked about (indiscernible)

00:48:16.560 --> 00:48:20.240
against Black indigeneity
and just this discourse

00:48:20.240 --> 00:48:23.090
around Blackness of
not belonging to place.

00:48:23.090 --> 00:48:26.960
And so, I'm curious if
there's also settler claims

00:48:26.960 --> 00:48:28.840
to indigeneity going on here

00:48:29.790 --> 00:48:32.400
with anyone else who's not
necessarily from Morocco

00:48:32.400 --> 00:48:37.400
who necessarily identify as
Black and still gets the access?

00:48:38.100 --> 00:48:39.260
- Right.

00:48:39.260 --> 00:48:44.260
So, there's a lot going
on that I understand some,

00:48:44.460 --> 00:48:46.620
but I'm really not the pers...

00:48:46.620 --> 00:48:48.360
So, I'm gonna gesture to some things,

00:48:48.360 --> 00:48:51.960
but I would not be the person
to unpack this in detail.

00:48:51.960 --> 00:48:55.170
But first, indigeneity is
kind of a weird concept.

00:48:55.170 --> 00:48:57.350
I think it translates kind of...

00:48:57.350 --> 00:48:59.990
I use that, but it translates strangely,

00:48:59.990 --> 00:49:02.020
partly because of the French.

00:49:02.020 --> 00:49:03.470
There's a little bit of
(speaking foreign language)

00:49:03.470 --> 00:49:06.880
happening there in Morocco.

00:49:06.880 --> 00:49:09.650
But this denial of Black indigeneity,

00:49:09.650 --> 00:49:12.750
or maybe Black (speaking
Arabic), identity,

00:49:12.750 --> 00:49:15.940
has to do with this
conflation with Blackness

00:49:15.940 --> 00:49:18.230
and slavery that Chouki
El Hamel talks about.

00:49:18.230 --> 00:49:19.510
So, the idea is, if you're Black,

00:49:19.510 --> 00:49:21.800
you're always from somewhere else.

00:49:21.800 --> 00:49:26.480
And very common, the most
common term that I heard

00:49:26.480 --> 00:49:27.720
when I lived in Morocco,

00:49:27.720 --> 00:49:29.340
that people would address Black people by

00:49:29.340 --> 00:49:32.070
was (speaking Arabic),
person from the South,

00:49:32.070 --> 00:49:34.080
I mean, from the Sahara.

00:49:34.080 --> 00:49:36.590
And so, this idea that you're
from another geography,

00:49:36.590 --> 00:49:41.590
from another place, would
deny your right to presence.

00:49:41.810 --> 00:49:43.950
But there's also this dynamic in Morocco,

00:49:43.950 --> 00:49:47.470
where Morocco itself is
a settler state, right,

00:49:47.470 --> 00:49:50.680
'cause it's occupying Western Sahara

00:49:50.680 --> 00:49:53.810
and there's all kinds of really
interesting racial dynamics

00:49:53.810 --> 00:49:55.773
in Western Sahara as well.

00:49:57.270 --> 00:49:58.380
So, yeah.

00:49:58.380 --> 00:49:59.650
So, there's a lot going on there

00:49:59.650 --> 00:50:02.350
that I think would be fascinating
for someone to unpack,

00:50:02.350 --> 00:50:06.763
but it's not the main
focus for me, right now.

00:50:07.810 --> 00:50:10.740
The region, the Tafilalet,
where this came out for me,

00:50:10.740 --> 00:50:15.170
is such an interesting and
diverse and varied region

00:50:15.170 --> 00:50:18.470
with a lot of history and culture

00:50:18.470 --> 00:50:22.400
that I haven't fully
apprehended, or investigated.

00:50:22.400 --> 00:50:26.080
And so, I think someone who
would do a really nuanced study

00:50:26.080 --> 00:50:28.950
of some of these
questions, would do well to

00:50:28.950 --> 00:50:30.570
kind of dive into that literature

00:50:30.570 --> 00:50:32.643
and think about it from that aspect.

00:50:33.980 --> 00:50:35.010
- [Jennifer] Thank you.

00:50:35.010 --> 00:50:36.123
- Yeah, no problem.

00:50:39.040 --> 00:50:41.810
Lydia, have I studied the
specificities of Blackness

00:50:41.810 --> 00:50:43.493
in Rabat, the capital?

00:50:44.370 --> 00:50:47.094
I haven't, I haven't, no.

00:50:47.094 --> 00:50:48.530
(Leslie chuckles)

00:50:48.530 --> 00:50:50.060
This is actually, in some ways,

00:50:50.060 --> 00:50:52.260
this paper is the follow-up to my research

00:50:52.260 --> 00:50:54.680
because the bulk of my
research is really focused

00:50:54.680 --> 00:50:59.680
on how the Euro-African border
produces migrants as Black

00:50:59.820 --> 00:51:02.770
in order to legitimize
violence against them

00:51:02.770 --> 00:51:05.840
and sort of to consign them
to this other place, right,

00:51:05.840 --> 00:51:06.673
to this

00:51:08.330 --> 00:51:10.440
zone of non-being, if you
wanna put it that way,

00:51:10.440 --> 00:51:11.870
or the un-geographic

00:51:11.870 --> 00:51:14.123
is how Katherine
McKittrick talks about it.

00:51:15.140 --> 00:51:16.400
And then I also think about

00:51:16.400 --> 00:51:18.500
it in terms of political economy.

00:51:18.500 --> 00:51:19.427
But the,

00:51:21.320 --> 00:51:24.150
the way that the border was
impacting Moroccan society

00:51:24.150 --> 00:51:26.460
more broadly, and I could
see this a little bit

00:51:26.460 --> 00:51:27.980
as kind of an outsider

00:51:27.980 --> 00:51:30.870
that was traveling and
living there frequently.

00:51:30.870 --> 00:51:33.000
I moved there the first time in 2001,

00:51:33.000 --> 00:51:36.363
just a few weeks before September
11th in the United States.

00:51:37.545 --> 00:51:41.400
And then returned periodically
for one or two year periods

00:51:42.370 --> 00:51:43.720
through 2017.

00:51:43.720 --> 00:51:45.920
So, I was able to see how this sort of

00:51:46.760 --> 00:51:51.540
this Blackness, illegality,
migrationist crisis,

00:51:51.540 --> 00:51:52.650
all of this discourse

00:51:52.650 --> 00:51:55.240
was becoming more and
more prominent in Morocco

00:51:55.240 --> 00:51:57.410
over the two decades or so that I've been

00:51:58.270 --> 00:52:00.603
living, or moving around there.

00:52:02.010 --> 00:52:05.500
So, definitely, you
know, I've heard things

00:52:05.500 --> 00:52:06.870
similar about Rabat,

00:52:06.870 --> 00:52:09.783
but I haven't done a
particular study of Rabat.

00:52:13.920 --> 00:52:15.717
Okay, Zeenab

00:52:17.000 --> 00:52:19.737
says, "I've read about how
Moroccan authorities have a habit

00:52:19.737 --> 00:52:23.207
"of quelling the formation
of anti-racist civil society

00:52:23.207 --> 00:52:25.710
"for a variety of possible reasons."

00:52:25.710 --> 00:52:27.260
Have I been pressured in any way,

00:52:27.260 --> 00:52:28.680
or approached by the authorities

00:52:28.680 --> 00:52:30.803
while conducting my research?

00:52:32.050 --> 00:52:34.384
No, that's, you know...

00:52:34.384 --> 00:52:37.530
So, one of my external
advisers is Michael Collier

00:52:37.530 --> 00:52:40.520
who's been doing research
on migration in Morocco

00:52:40.520 --> 00:52:43.590
in the region since 1997.

00:52:43.590 --> 00:52:46.480
And he's really careful to emphasize

00:52:46.480 --> 00:52:49.510
that one of the reasons we're
able to talk about Morocco

00:52:49.510 --> 00:52:54.220
as a violent border state
or, racism in Morocco,

00:52:54.220 --> 00:52:55.053
or things like that,

00:52:55.053 --> 00:52:57.490
is in part because they allow researchers

00:52:57.490 --> 00:53:00.350
to come and ask some of these questions.

00:53:00.350 --> 00:53:04.130
So, you know, you can't do
this research in Algeria

00:53:04.130 --> 00:53:07.663
as easily, at least as an
American, as a U.S. citizen.

00:53:11.350 --> 00:53:14.270
So, in that sense, no,
I haven't been limited.

00:53:14.270 --> 00:53:18.120
In another sense, you do have
to get a research permit.

00:53:18.120 --> 00:53:21.050
So, I was encouraged
by my host organization

00:53:21.050 --> 00:53:24.180
to be really careful about
how I framed these questions

00:53:24.180 --> 00:53:26.380
and to speak in generalities.

00:53:26.380 --> 00:53:29.770
In fact, my partner was
doing historical research

00:53:29.770 --> 00:53:32.330
on the Rif, which is
sort of a much more...

00:53:33.831 --> 00:53:36.480
It was right in the
midst of the Herach Rifi

00:53:36.480 --> 00:53:37.860
kind of movement there

00:53:37.860 --> 00:53:40.693
and he wasn't given a
research permit and in fact,

00:53:42.240 --> 00:53:44.023
was told not to do research.

00:53:45.390 --> 00:53:46.950
So, I think it varies by topic.

00:53:46.950 --> 00:53:51.200
But Morocco has been fairly
accommodating to people

00:53:51.200 --> 00:53:54.010
who are doing research
on migration despite

00:53:54.010 --> 00:53:57.283
most of that research being
pretty strong in critique.

00:54:14.080 --> 00:54:16.110
- [Female Speaker] If we
have a couple of minutes,

00:54:16.110 --> 00:54:18.530
if there's not other questions,
I'm interested to know

00:54:18.530 --> 00:54:22.740
a bit more about this political
economy that you mentioned.

00:54:22.740 --> 00:54:24.510
And I think that was

00:54:24.510 --> 00:54:27.820
a big part of your research and

00:54:29.130 --> 00:54:32.260
how it feeds into this whole situation?

00:54:32.260 --> 00:54:33.240
- Right, yeah.

00:54:33.240 --> 00:54:35.640
So, you know, in some ways

00:54:35.640 --> 00:54:39.310
I'm still puzzling that
out a little bit, but

00:54:41.440 --> 00:54:43.510
there's an anthropologist,
Ruben Andersson.

00:54:43.510 --> 00:54:45.360
There've been some other people as well,

00:54:45.360 --> 00:54:47.230
but he really wrote about

00:54:50.380 --> 00:54:52.870
the border, like this transnational border

00:54:52.870 --> 00:54:55.970
between Europe and Africa as an industry

00:54:55.970 --> 00:54:59.950
that exists to kind of circulate capital

00:54:59.950 --> 00:55:01.440
based on the illegality,

00:55:01.440 --> 00:55:04.450
or the production of
illegality of migrants.

00:55:04.450 --> 00:55:06.480
And other scholars have done that too.

00:55:06.480 --> 00:55:10.920
So, the idea is, again, that
the border can only exist

00:55:10.920 --> 00:55:14.280
as long as you're criminalizing
people who are moving

00:55:14.280 --> 00:55:19.243
and beyond that, that it
feeds, it feeds itself by,

00:55:24.890 --> 00:55:26.700
through the accumulation of capital.

00:55:26.700 --> 00:55:29.210
I'll give an example to be less abstract.

00:55:29.210 --> 00:55:34.064
So, basically when Europe
infuses money into,

00:55:34.064 --> 00:55:35.850
grants big defense contractors...

00:55:35.850 --> 00:55:37.480
In the 1990s, you have to remember,

00:55:37.480 --> 00:55:40.570
this is after the fall of the Berlin Wall

00:55:40.570 --> 00:55:42.830
and the Cold War was
sort of officially over

00:55:42.830 --> 00:55:45.640
and so suddenly you had all
these defense contractors

00:55:45.640 --> 00:55:49.700
who didn't have markets
anymore to sell their,

00:55:49.700 --> 00:55:52.150
to sell their wares, or their weapons

00:55:52.150 --> 00:55:55.150
and their technologies,
so they repackage them

00:55:55.150 --> 00:55:59.020
for civilian use and have advertised them

00:55:59.020 --> 00:56:03.130
as being able to track migrants
and to protect migrants,

00:56:03.130 --> 00:56:05.770
is one of the ways, one of
the languages that they use.

00:56:05.770 --> 00:56:10.190
So, that sort of has kept the
defense industry for a while.

00:56:10.190 --> 00:56:13.240
That kept them going for
a while and it's become,

00:56:13.240 --> 00:56:15.660
you know, the expenditures
have become higher and higher.

00:56:15.660 --> 00:56:18.820
And I would point to the
Transnational Institute

00:56:18.820 --> 00:56:20.450
and Mark Akkerman,

00:56:20.450 --> 00:56:22.620
who is someone who's
done research on that.

00:56:22.620 --> 00:56:24.650
And then of as borders get stronger

00:56:24.650 --> 00:56:27.950
and it gets harder to get
across, you'd have smugglers

00:56:27.950 --> 00:56:32.410
who are also suddenly not
making a little bit of money,

00:56:32.410 --> 00:56:33.850
just helping someone,

00:56:33.850 --> 00:56:36.100
directing them through
the desert somewhere,

00:56:36.100 --> 00:56:40.300
but suddenly having to
buy gear and night vision

00:56:40.300 --> 00:56:43.110
and all of these things
and also can charge

00:56:43.110 --> 00:56:46.260
a lot more money to migrants
and refugees who wanna cross

00:56:46.260 --> 00:56:47.690
because it's gotten a lot more dangerous

00:56:47.690 --> 00:56:50.032
and they need their specialized expertise.

00:56:50.032 --> 00:56:54.990
So, there's a certain group
in the south of Algeria

00:56:54.990 --> 00:56:57.880
who, I think, during the refugee crisis of

00:56:58.740 --> 00:57:02.090
2015, said they made something
like a billion dollars.

00:57:02.090 --> 00:57:03.860
I mean, I don't know if it's true or not,

00:57:03.860 --> 00:57:06.690
but there's tremendous money in that.

00:57:06.690 --> 00:57:09.530
So, what I do is try to link that to race

00:57:09.530 --> 00:57:12.260
and think about it through
racial capitalist lens,

00:57:12.260 --> 00:57:15.070
where it's not just the
production of illegality,

00:57:15.070 --> 00:57:18.340
criminalizing people, but it's
also the production of race

00:57:18.340 --> 00:57:22.023
that, making people who are
Black and are on the move,

00:57:24.000 --> 00:57:28.970
targets of the border, both
defense contractors, states,

00:57:28.970 --> 00:57:31.930
but also humanitarian
agencies and also smugglers,

00:57:31.930 --> 00:57:35.063
all of these people are trying
to get a piece of the pie.

00:57:37.270 --> 00:57:39.730
It's driven by sort of a racial logic.

00:57:39.730 --> 00:57:43.710
So, that's where I connect
those two things together.

00:57:43.710 --> 00:57:45.490
And I'm still thinking
through how that works

00:57:45.490 --> 00:57:50.410
and what the utility of a
racial capitalist idea is,

00:57:50.410 --> 00:57:51.510
but I think it also...

00:57:51.510 --> 00:57:53.320
Political economy we often think of

00:57:53.320 --> 00:57:56.410
as centered around labor and
I don't think this is centered

00:57:56.410 --> 00:57:57.270
just around labor.

00:57:57.270 --> 00:58:01.290
It's not just about bringing
people from Africa to Europe

00:58:01.290 --> 00:58:02.610
in precarious condition

00:58:02.610 --> 00:58:04.720
and being to able to exploit their labor,

00:58:04.720 --> 00:58:06.780
but it's actually just
exploiting their movements

00:58:06.780 --> 00:58:09.800
and their bodies and
making money off of that.

00:58:09.800 --> 00:58:11.893
So, that's what I'm focused on.

00:58:13.000 --> 00:58:14.050
Does that make sense?

00:58:17.030 --> 00:58:17.863
Go ahead.

00:58:17.863 --> 00:58:18.696
- Hey, Leslie.

00:58:20.121 --> 00:58:22.040
First of all, I'm so sorry

00:58:22.040 --> 00:58:24.150
'cause I missed the bulk of your talk

00:58:24.150 --> 00:58:26.700
and I think could just get
last five minutes 'cause

00:58:28.040 --> 00:58:29.470
I had actually prior meeting.

00:58:29.470 --> 00:58:32.530
But I find this really
fascinating just from the Q&amp;A

00:58:32.530 --> 00:58:33.930
and the question that are being asked.

00:58:33.930 --> 00:58:35.640
But I just wanna...

00:58:35.640 --> 00:58:40.120
Probably my question more on your research

00:58:40.120 --> 00:58:42.810
and I find that, and
you just mentioned this,

00:58:42.810 --> 00:58:47.713
my understanding is that
lately there has been

00:58:47.713 --> 00:58:51.340
sort of strict rules actually
in getting your permission

00:58:51.340 --> 00:58:54.170
as far as sort of migration, because,

00:58:54.170 --> 00:58:56.310
and you actually mentioned
this just earlier,

00:58:56.310 --> 00:58:59.470
the fact that it's sort of paradoxical

00:58:59.470 --> 00:59:01.880
that the state would give you permission

00:59:01.880 --> 00:59:04.810
and you have to go through
all these loopholes.

00:59:04.810 --> 00:59:07.650
However, a lot of the
research that's been produced

00:59:07.650 --> 00:59:09.210
is sort of scathing to the state,

00:59:09.210 --> 00:59:12.343
or sort of scathing critique of the state.

00:59:13.580 --> 00:59:16.030
And I'd like just to hear
a little bit from you

00:59:16.030 --> 00:59:19.913
as far as the generalization
that you've mentioned,

00:59:20.944 --> 00:59:23.610
sort of to get research research permit,

00:59:23.610 --> 00:59:28.143
you have to somewhat tweak your questions,

00:59:29.484 --> 00:59:31.040
which can be problematic sometimes

00:59:31.040 --> 00:59:33.430
'cause there's always this
sort of like, oh, you...

00:59:33.430 --> 00:59:34.920
And I know this very well.

00:59:34.920 --> 00:59:39.340
I know people that wanted to
do research in Western Sahara

00:59:39.340 --> 00:59:40.980
and they would not even be allowed

00:59:40.980 --> 00:59:42.700
to actually even come close to there,

00:59:42.700 --> 00:59:45.240
or even have any sort of interlocutors,

00:59:45.240 --> 00:59:46.790
which is really, really hard.

00:59:46.790 --> 00:59:48.610
Anybody that would

00:59:48.610 --> 00:59:50.620
even envision they would
do the research there

00:59:50.620 --> 00:59:51.960
would not be even...

00:59:51.960 --> 00:59:54.530
I know someone that tried to,

00:59:54.530 --> 00:59:57.090
sort of tried to work on
divorce and tweak things

00:59:57.090 --> 00:59:59.243
to be able to get even to that territory.

01:00:00.380 --> 01:00:03.720
Sort of long-winded, but could you,

01:00:03.720 --> 01:00:05.540
you know, could you say a bit about it

01:00:05.540 --> 01:00:07.460
because it's really sometimes so hard

01:00:07.460 --> 01:00:09.240
because also when you're doing research

01:00:09.240 --> 01:00:11.430
and you want to get your research permit,

01:00:11.430 --> 01:00:14.320
there are all these committees
and things you go through

01:00:14.320 --> 01:00:16.213
that you have to sort of say

01:00:16.213 --> 01:00:18.180
here is what I'm really doing.

01:00:18.180 --> 01:00:20.830
And at times you might also have to...

01:00:21.890 --> 01:00:23.860
I think about Egypt, just
to give you an example.

01:00:23.860 --> 01:00:26.823
You talk about Algeria where
at times people even having

01:00:27.980 --> 01:00:29.540
you know, one had to
apply for the Fulbright

01:00:29.540 --> 01:00:32.040
and actually get the
Fulbright, they were rejected

01:00:32.040 --> 01:00:34.200
because there are certain questions that

01:00:34.200 --> 01:00:36.280
the way they formulated the questions

01:00:37.863 --> 01:00:40.900
sort of was not welcomed by
the committee, or by the state.

01:00:40.900 --> 01:00:43.250
I just wanna hear a little bit about,

01:00:43.250 --> 01:00:45.300
especially that you've spent
a lot of time in the Morocco

01:00:45.300 --> 01:00:47.210
back and forth with your partner

01:00:47.210 --> 01:00:50.690
and doing lot of work there,

01:00:50.690 --> 01:00:52.600
are there any...

01:00:52.600 --> 01:00:56.720
Probably you had a very,
you know, very clear road.

01:00:56.720 --> 01:00:57.980
You didn't have a lot of obstacles

01:00:57.980 --> 01:01:00.650
also probably because of your affiliation.

01:01:00.650 --> 01:01:05.510
But I wanna hear more, this
sort of tension between,

01:01:05.510 --> 01:01:08.410
yes, you can get your permit, however,

01:01:08.410 --> 01:01:10.900
it's a very oppressive
system that's really careful

01:01:10.900 --> 01:01:12.980
about what people write about it

01:01:12.980 --> 01:01:14.920
and still there's scathing critique.

01:01:14.920 --> 01:01:18.080
I hope I'm making sense.
- Yeah, totally, totally.

01:01:18.080 --> 01:01:20.840
I think I'll answer that question.

01:01:20.840 --> 01:01:24.580
I'll zoom out a little bit
to sort of ethics in general

01:01:25.421 --> 01:01:28.170
and also just my position.

01:01:28.170 --> 01:01:31.460
Not only am I a white American,

01:01:31.460 --> 01:01:35.200
but I had a research affiliation
with a very prominent

01:01:36.660 --> 01:01:39.350
university in Morocco,
Al Akhawayn University

01:01:39.350 --> 01:01:41.590
and I have a long history with them.

01:01:41.590 --> 01:01:45.730
So, I was leveraging a lot of my privilege

01:01:45.730 --> 01:01:48.953
that other researchers
might not have access to.

01:01:49.950 --> 01:01:51.998
You know, if you're South African,

01:01:51.998 --> 01:01:52.831
(male speaker chuckles)
you're not,

01:01:52.831 --> 01:01:55.030
they're not gonna let
you come to the country

01:01:55.030 --> 01:01:58.740
probably much less,
must less to do research

01:01:58.740 --> 01:02:00.813
and there's lots of other examples.

01:02:02.876 --> 01:02:03.709
So, I think that was part of it.

01:02:03.709 --> 01:02:05.180
And actually I mentioned talking

01:02:05.180 --> 01:02:08.140
to the three Meknesi researchers

01:02:09.360 --> 01:02:10.820
when I was first starting and I was like,

01:02:10.820 --> 01:02:13.940
well, how do you go about getting access,

01:02:13.940 --> 01:02:16.860
you know, going into the
camps and stuff like that?

01:02:16.860 --> 01:02:18.840
And they said, oh,
you're a white American.

01:02:18.840 --> 01:02:21.080
No problem, you just
walk right in, you know?

01:02:21.080 --> 01:02:25.610
And so, there were ways
in which that position,

01:02:25.610 --> 01:02:28.003
for better or worse
and probably for worse,

01:02:29.260 --> 01:02:30.780
enabled me to do that.

01:02:30.780 --> 01:02:33.490
In terms of generalities,
I'll be really honest.

01:02:33.490 --> 01:02:34.730
I'm an over-discloser,

01:02:34.730 --> 01:02:37.470
if you haven't noticed
that already in this talk.

01:02:37.470 --> 01:02:41.580
And I wrote out very
detailed research proposal

01:02:41.580 --> 01:02:44.190
for the Ministry of Foreign Affairs

01:02:44.190 --> 01:02:47.300
and the Ministry of Interior
and then somebody else

01:02:47.300 --> 01:02:49.560
that was in the sponsoring institution,

01:02:49.560 --> 01:02:50.905
edited it for me.

01:02:50.905 --> 01:02:53.090
(both laughing)
so.

01:02:53.090 --> 01:02:55.890
And you know, probably had
a better eye for the sort of

01:02:55.890 --> 01:02:57.910
political nature
(indiscernible)

01:02:57.910 --> 01:02:59.350
of how to act and how I should do it.

01:02:59.350 --> 01:03:01.320
But I mean, I think there's an ethics too,

01:03:01.320 --> 01:03:04.150
to what you disclose and
what you don't disclose

01:03:04.150 --> 01:03:05.880
and how you...

01:03:05.880 --> 01:03:06.713
Yeah.

01:03:06.713 --> 01:03:09.200
And also what you go in expecting to see.

01:03:09.200 --> 01:03:10.840
I think my situation
was a little different

01:03:10.840 --> 01:03:13.200
because I'd been in Morocco for so long

01:03:13.200 --> 01:03:14.750
and had already encountered

01:03:16.260 --> 01:03:18.290
the experience of being with migrants

01:03:19.616 --> 01:03:20.800
who were experiencing violence

01:03:20.800 --> 01:03:23.180
and over-policing and things like that,

01:03:23.180 --> 01:03:26.330
so I kind of knew what I
was probably gonna find.

01:03:26.330 --> 01:03:27.980
But I think we also
have to think about that

01:03:27.980 --> 01:03:31.330
and be really careful for
going in, expecting to find

01:03:32.530 --> 01:03:35.780
racism, or expecting to
overlay our particular

01:03:38.070 --> 01:03:41.010
epistemological orientation
onto a new context.

01:03:41.010 --> 01:03:44.650
You know, that can be
problematic as well, so.

01:03:44.650 --> 01:03:47.160
Yeah, so, I think it is something...

01:03:47.160 --> 01:03:48.640
And I think it was helpful for me

01:03:48.640 --> 01:03:51.280
that I was affiliated with
a Moroccan institution,

01:03:51.280 --> 01:03:52.620
not just for the,

01:03:52.620 --> 01:03:55.860
you know, for the kind
of greasing the wheels,

01:03:55.860 --> 01:03:58.270
but also because it kept me
a little bit more accountable

01:03:58.270 --> 01:04:00.560
to scholars who were doing similar work,

01:04:00.560 --> 01:04:01.730
who were from the country,

01:04:01.730 --> 01:04:04.270
or who were long-term
residents of the country.

01:04:04.270 --> 01:04:09.270
So, I didn't feel like I was
quite so much of a lone cowboy

01:04:09.630 --> 01:04:12.560
doing a lot of this work
and had some accountability,

01:04:12.560 --> 01:04:15.550
not just to my research interlocutors,

01:04:15.550 --> 01:04:18.400
Western Central African
migrants, but also to other folks

01:04:19.820 --> 01:04:21.990
in the region who were experts on this

01:04:21.990 --> 01:04:24.470
and were asking me questions

01:04:24.470 --> 01:04:27.280
and talking through these
issues with me as well.

01:04:27.280 --> 01:04:29.540
- [Male Speaker] Thank
you so much, Leslie.

01:04:29.540 --> 01:04:30.390
The reason really a way to ask you that

01:04:30.390 --> 01:04:32.590
'cause I know someone
who recently actually

01:04:32.590 --> 01:04:36.783
wanted to do similar work
and it was actually rejected.

01:04:36.783 --> 01:04:37.616
But you're right.

01:04:37.616 --> 01:04:39.810
The whole politics of
access and affiliation,

01:04:39.810 --> 01:04:41.630
I mean, it absolutely makes total sense.

01:04:41.630 --> 01:04:42.490
Thank you.

01:04:42.490 --> 01:04:43.323
- No problem.

01:04:43.323 --> 01:04:45.960
And I will say, I think Morocco
is getting a little stricter

01:04:45.960 --> 01:04:48.123
on research permits, so that is true.

01:04:49.634 --> 01:04:52.670
- There is a question from Liz Chrismar

01:04:53.610 --> 01:04:56.783
about the data (indiscernible)

01:04:56.783 --> 01:04:59.400
(indiscernible) and also (indiscernible)

01:04:59.400 --> 01:05:01.790
So, if you can reply
to these two questions,

01:05:01.790 --> 01:05:04.423
I think this will be the last two ones.

01:05:06.220 --> 01:05:07.053
- Okay.

01:05:07.053 --> 01:05:10.830
So Liz, I don't know if I can talk about

01:05:10.830 --> 01:05:13.840
the externalization of
the border in Morocco

01:05:13.840 --> 01:05:16.990
and how it plays out through
technology and data collection.

01:05:16.990 --> 01:05:21.510
In part, because a lot of the...

01:05:21.510 --> 01:05:24.680
From the state perspective,
the Ministry of Interior,

01:05:24.680 --> 01:05:27.570
is the one who keeps the
most accurate, and I'm sure,

01:05:27.570 --> 01:05:31.083
exhaustive data on this and
they're not telling anyone.

01:05:33.150 --> 01:05:34.940
So, that's sort of a black box.

01:05:34.940 --> 01:05:36.620
I know that in terms of UNHCR

01:05:38.380 --> 01:05:42.260
migrants are fingerprinted
now, and that has to do with

01:05:42.260 --> 01:05:44.530
where people can make asylum claims

01:05:44.530 --> 01:05:48.570
and this push to get people
to seek asylum in Morocco

01:05:48.570 --> 01:05:52.700
and other third countries
rather than in Europe itself.

01:05:52.700 --> 01:05:55.150
So, those are the two things I really know

01:05:55.150 --> 01:05:57.400
about data in Morocco that
hasn't been something.

01:05:57.400 --> 01:06:00.130
Because my research was so
ethnographic among migrants

01:06:00.130 --> 01:06:01.080
it wasn't something

01:06:02.800 --> 01:06:04.120
that I encountered that much,

01:06:04.120 --> 01:06:07.080
except sort of death by paperwork.

01:06:07.080 --> 01:06:10.780
In especially the regularization process,

01:06:10.780 --> 01:06:13.350
you had to submit a ton of papers.

01:06:13.350 --> 01:06:14.730
You had to get a police report

01:06:14.730 --> 01:06:16.440
and if you could find
your birth certificate

01:06:16.440 --> 01:06:17.310
and all of those things.

01:06:17.310 --> 01:06:20.140
And from my perspective, that
was a really effective way

01:06:20.140 --> 01:06:23.080
to get more information
and better sense of

01:06:23.080 --> 01:06:26.960
who migrants were, where they
are from, how many there were,

01:06:26.960 --> 01:06:28.460
and just keep track of people.

01:06:28.460 --> 01:06:31.060
So, I do see the regularization program

01:06:31.920 --> 01:06:34.920
as also being a way to
keep that data, but again,

01:06:34.920 --> 01:06:37.290
it went to the Ministry of Interior

01:06:37.290 --> 01:06:39.930
and I don't know who
they're sharing it with.

01:06:39.930 --> 01:06:42.947
And then Iman, "Have there
been moments in your field work

01:06:42.947 --> 01:06:44.927
"where you were emotionally affected

01:06:44.927 --> 01:06:46.430
"by what you've seen and heard?"

01:06:46.430 --> 01:06:50.160
Yes.
(Leslie chuckles)

01:06:50.160 --> 01:06:52.133
Yes, there definitely were.

01:06:53.580 --> 01:06:57.460
But I would say one thing is
that the people that I was with

01:06:57.460 --> 01:06:58.803
were so,

01:06:59.760 --> 01:07:01.360
were just living their lives, right?

01:07:01.360 --> 01:07:06.360
Western Central African
migrants had really hard times.

01:07:06.470 --> 01:07:08.150
Things were often really bad,

01:07:08.150 --> 01:07:09.510
but they were also.
(bird banging)

01:07:09.510 --> 01:07:10.500
Oh my gosh! Sorry.

01:07:10.500 --> 01:07:14.550
A bird just hit the window.
(Leslie chuckles)

01:07:14.550 --> 01:07:17.200
Things were really bad, but they were also

01:07:20.150 --> 01:07:24.520
not victims, right, in sort
of this unidimensional sense.

01:07:24.520 --> 01:07:26.110
And so, being with them

01:07:28.896 --> 01:07:32.960
and that sense of community
that was in those communities,

01:07:32.960 --> 01:07:34.730
always lightened the load.

01:07:34.730 --> 01:07:36.990
I mean, that's the best
way that I can say it.

01:07:36.990 --> 01:07:40.040
And not just, I think,
for me, but also for them.

01:07:40.040 --> 01:07:43.020
I think where I felt
heavier was when I came back

01:07:43.020 --> 01:07:45.670
and was going back through my
field notes and writing it up

01:07:45.670 --> 01:07:46.730
and I found writing...

01:07:46.730 --> 01:07:48.180
I actually...

01:07:48.180 --> 01:07:51.040
One, I found writing it up
really hard, but second,

01:07:51.040 --> 01:07:54.060
I decided not to include
very many examples at all

01:07:54.060 --> 01:07:55.050
for my field work.

01:07:55.050 --> 01:07:57.550
So, a lot of my book manuscript

01:07:57.550 --> 01:08:02.320
is drawing on just a few
anecdotes and unpacking them

01:08:02.320 --> 01:08:04.380
one, because I don't wanna expose people

01:08:04.380 --> 01:08:06.003
in that particular way,

01:08:07.130 --> 01:08:09.680
especially when it's
sometimes at their hardest,

01:08:09.680 --> 01:08:11.950
or their lowest spot.

01:08:11.950 --> 01:08:13.619
And also because

01:08:13.619 --> 01:08:15.940
I think there's been
plenty of documentation

01:08:15.940 --> 01:08:17.100
about how hard it is

01:08:17.100 --> 01:08:20.100
to be a Western Central
African migrant on the move.

01:08:20.100 --> 01:08:24.250
And you don't need more
specificity from me on that.

01:08:24.250 --> 01:08:26.510
So, that's kind of how I dealt with that.

01:08:26.510 --> 01:08:28.130
I should also just add that

01:08:29.880 --> 01:08:32.140
really the reason that
I came to this project,

01:08:32.140 --> 01:08:33.770
it wasn't my original intention,

01:08:33.770 --> 01:08:37.290
but having lived in Morocco,
I just met migrants.

01:08:37.290 --> 01:08:40.820
And I was a member of the
Protestant Church in Morocco,

01:08:40.820 --> 01:08:42.570
which became...

01:08:42.570 --> 01:08:44.600
Really started diverting
a lot of its attention

01:08:44.600 --> 01:08:46.150
to helping migrants

01:08:46.150 --> 01:08:48.320
many of whom were
filling the congregations

01:08:48.320 --> 01:08:49.430
across the country.

01:08:49.430 --> 01:08:53.140
And so, for me, it was really just about

01:08:53.140 --> 01:08:55.700
kind of looking around at where I'd been

01:08:55.700 --> 01:08:58.140
and what was happening there in a country

01:08:58.140 --> 01:09:00.120
that I love, I love it.

01:09:00.120 --> 01:09:02.010
My first child was born in Morocco

01:09:02.010 --> 01:09:04.780
and it's a place that's
really special to me.

01:09:04.780 --> 01:09:06.663
But I wanted to think more about,

01:09:09.270 --> 01:09:12.120
what these changes are that
I've witnessed over two decades

01:09:12.120 --> 01:09:13.220
were really about

01:09:14.064 --> 01:09:16.450
and also to locate a
lot of that culpability,

01:09:16.450 --> 01:09:19.340
or responsibility in
Europe and more broadly

01:09:19.340 --> 01:09:22.610
in white supremacy and the
legacies of colonialism

01:09:22.610 --> 01:09:23.900
and racial capitalism.

01:09:23.900 --> 01:09:25.160
So, that's kind of,

01:09:25.160 --> 01:09:27.110
that's what brought me to this project.

